      "title": "Building Quality Inspector",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position. Located at the Services Building (G12) on the Darlington Campus Ensure the successful delivery of major infrastructure projects at the University of Sydney Competitive salary package on offer including 17% Superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for an experienced and motivated Building Quality Inspector to join the Infrastructure Delivery team. The Building Quality Inspector (BQI) plays a critical role in the successful delivery of major infrastructure projects at the University of Sydney. Acting as the University’s on-site representative, the BQI provides independent oversight of construction activities to ensure the highest standards of quality, safety, and compliance are maintained throughout project delivery. The role is responsible for monitoring construction works against approved designs, specifications, relevant standards, and regulatory requirements. Through rigorous inspections, documentation, and reporting, the BQI identifies and addresses potential issues early, minimises defects and rework, protects project integrity, and supports the delivery of high-quality outcomes that provide long-term value for the University. The Building Quality Inspector (BQI) will work closely with project teams, contractors, and key stakeholders to ensure construction activities are delivered safely, efficiently, and to the required standards of quality and compliance. The successful candidate will be based on-site and responsible for overseeing construction projects across the University of Sydney’s Darlington and Camperdown campuses. Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct daily inspections of construction activities to ensure compliance with design specifications, engineering drawings, and quality standards monitor the quality of workmanship and materials used, identifying any deviations or substandard practices verify that all construction activities comply with relevant building codes, statutory regulations, and University policies conduct safety inspections and identify potential hazards, unsafe practices, or non-compliant equipment. detect and document construction defects or poor workmanship early in the process to prevent rework and delays provide clear, concise, and timely updates to the Project Director to support informed decision-making review construction drawings, specifications, and technical documents to ensure clarity and feasibility engage with contractors to understand their three-week look-ahead schedules and prepare for upcoming inspections. About you accredited/licensed building trade qualification or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience minimum 20 years of relevant experience in building construction, quality assurance, and regulatory compliance proven track record in site inspection, defect identification, and construction oversight experience working with diverse trades on complex infrastructure projects in major-level health, research or education buildings working knowledge of WHS responsibilities and risk management, obligations and applying SafeWork NSW legislation & regulation into the workplace extensive knowledge of regulatory requirements in the building industry (including NSW Legislation, BCA, Australian Standards) as well as technical knowledge of professional disciplines effective communication skills (both verbal and written) with ability to interact, influence and negotiate with a broad range of people within and outside the University with a high level of tact, diplomacy, and discretion, including the ability to manage people who may be in distress or difficulty deep understanding of construction inspection processes and quality assurance. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please address the “about you” section in your cover letter with relevant examples. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 15 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Volunteers-Officer_0155600-3": {
      "title": "Volunteers Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nContract role until September 2027 Reporting to the Global Volunteers Manager, the Volunteers Officer (O.8 FTE) supports the delivery of a global volunteer program for alumni and supporters of the University of Sydney. The Volunteers Officer provides support to the Global Volunteers team including a combination of volunteer campaign management, frontline stakeholder liaison, event coordination support and data administration.   The role will see you working 4 days a week to provide proactive and timely management of inbound client/stakeholder enquiries and outbound client/stakeholder contact. The Volunteers Officer will drive the delivery of effective volunteer engagement campaigns, including recruitment and recognition of volunteers, as well as undertaking day to day tasks associated with the provision of frontline stakeholder enquiry management and project administration. This includes assisting with documentation and tracking of projects, updating databases, sourcing information, corresponding with internal and external stakeholders, and producing reports.   About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a part time Volunteers Officer (4 days per week) who: has exceptional interpersonal skills is a good stakeholder manager and relationship builder, ideally from working in large and complex environments has project management and administrative skills in order to monitor volunteer recruitment, training, retention and recognition has experience with the processes and governance involved with volunteer committees is well organised to be able to provide project support and database management and reporting is highly creative and can generate ideas and think outside the box. We’re always looking at ways to creatively engage our most valued supporters is a team player who can work with the wider alumni and supporter experience team has exceptional organisational and time-management skills, including the capacity to manage competing priorities, maintain attention to detail and to meet deadlines. Ideally you have experience working with digital campaigns and volunteers and committees, this is advantageous but not essential.   About us This role and sits within the Global Campaigns and Volunteers team . This team is responsible for driving mass connections with alumni and supporters through domestic and international campaigns, and best-practice volunteering models. The scope of the team includes planning and sending communications to alumni, providing data lists to internal stakeholders and identifying and recruiting volunteers for different types of opportunities. This includes partnering with other parts of the University, such as the student life and international student recruitment teams to help refer alumni volunteers and expand the range of volunteering opportunities. This team also works with alumni networks across the world to help them connect with peers locally and stay in touch with the University. The wider team is the Alumni and Supporter Experience team which engages with alumni and the broader community through a range of communications, events, community giving and engagement programs. The University of Sydney has a community of over 420,000 alumni with alumni associations and networks around the globe.   To express interest in this opportunity please complete the online application including:    a copy of your resume cover letter AND address the 2 selection criteria in the application.   Other Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks to be satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position.   If you are a current employee of the University and would like to submit an application, please login into your Workday account.   Navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard once logged into Workday and click on the USYD Find Jobs report to apply.   The University of Sydney is committed to diversity and social inclusion. Applications from people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds; equity target groups including women, people with disabilities, people who identify as LGBTIQ; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged.   For a confidential discussion about this role, please contact Sarah Scott, Recruitment Operations, via email, sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 15 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lead-Scientist_0150303": {
      "title": "Lead Scientist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFlexible job share opportunity (2 positions FTE negotiable) until 31 December 2028 for 2 experienced scientists provide leadership and operational oversight for an established translational research focused laboratory Up to 1 FTE combined Salary $131,108 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Centre for Organ Assessment, Repair & Optimisation (COARO) is seeking two experienced scientists to provide complementary scientific leadership and day-to-day operational oversight of an established translational research laboratory focused on ex vivo perfusion of human organs. This is an ideal opportunity for established scientists seeking flexible arrangements without compromising leadership impact. The two positions are designed as a co-leadership model, with complementary expertise that strengthens the lab's research direction. The laboratory has developed a world-leading platform enabling long-term ex vivo organ preservation for up to 7 weeks under near-physiological conditions, creating a unique environment in which organ repair, regeneration, and metabolic adaptation can be established over extended, clinically meaningful timescales. These roles represent the first dedicated biological leadership positions within the program, charged with defining the mechanistic framework underpinning sustained organ repair and regeneration. Key research areas include understanding the changes in organ metabolism during extended ex vivo perfusion, organ repair, remodelling and regeneration, with a focus on liver, but extending also to other organs such as kidney, pancreas, etc. This laboratory is well funded and entering a growth phase, including the world's first clinical trial of long-term organ preservation prior to transplantation. The Two Complementary Positions Position 1: Cellular Metabolism, Mitochondria Physiology You bring deep scientific expertise in metabolism, mitochondrial function and physiology. You will apply this knowledge to understand metabolic and physiological changes occurring during extended ex vivo organ preservation, translating your expertise to this novel context. Position 2: Cellular Repair/Regeneration You bring deep scientific expertise in tissue repair mechanisms, regeneration biology, and cellular recovery pathways. You will apply this knowledge to understand organ regeneration and functional recovery in ex vivo systems, translating your expertise to this innovative platform. Flexible Job Share Arrangement The two positions are offered as a flexible job share arrangement to be negotiated with successful candidates. Combined, they equate to up to 1 FTE, but the distribution is flexible and will be agreed during appointment. What You Will Do (Shared Responsibilities) Provide intellectual leadership and scientific expertise specific to your domain (metabolism/physiology OR regeneration/repair) Lead and physically conduct experiments, including experimental design, execution, optimisation, and troubleshooting Process and interpret samples using basic science techniques (Western blot, microscopy, etc.) and multiomic data (lipidomics, metabolomics, transcriptomics) Co-oversee the scientific agenda, helping design experiments, define priorities, hypotheses, and assessment strategies Provide supervision and oversight for the smooth running of multiple research streams conducted at the lab Supervise PhD students and junior researchers, ensuring mentorship across the laboratory Ensure smooth day-to-day laboratory operations, planning, and coordination Work collaboratively with engineers, scientists, and laboratory staff in a multidisciplinary environment Contribute to publications, reporting, and the longer-term development of the laboratory Maintain oversight of the organisation and progress of research projects across the laboratory Integrate experimental data across projects to develop coherent biological insight About You Essential Qualifications PhD with postdoctoral research experience demonstrating scientific independence Demonstrated deep expertise in one of the following domains (with working knowledge or the ability to upskill across others): Cellular metabolism and injury Cellular regeneration and repair mechanisms Mitochondrial biology and function Experience & Skills Evidence of publication in high-impact journals demonstrating research quality and productivity Demonstrated ability to design, execute, and interpret complex experiments (shown by peer-reviewed publications) Proven ability to work with multiomic datasets and integrate diverse analytical approaches Track record of leading or contributing to sustained, multi-phase experimental programs Experience working in translational or clinically adjacent research environments, ideally involving human tissue or organ systems Demonstrated capacity to integrate experimental data across projects to develop coherent biological insight Demonstrated ability to prioritise work, maintain a strategic overview of multiple projects, and attend to experimental detail simultaneously Outstanding organisational and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary teams Desirable Track record of co-leadership or collaborative leadership models Experience successfully balancing leadership with hands-on laboratory work Commitment to mentoring early-career researchers To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 14 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Clinical-Trials-Assistant--Targeted-_0156113": {
      "title": "Clinical Trials Assistant (Targeted)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 12-month fixed-term, based at the Chris O’Brien Lifehouse on the lands of the Gadigal people of the Eora Nation Exciting opportunity for a Clinical Trial Assistant to join the Sydney Clinical Trials Centre (CTC) at the University of Sydney Base Salary $89,755 - $100,495 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977. The Sydney CTC is a flagship centre of excellence in clinical trials and related research at the University of Sydney. We design and deliver trials and related research, including coordination, monitoring, data acquisition and management, and statistical analysis. Our health economics, biostatistics, systematic reviews, and biomarker teams work with trial data to inform healthcare providers about best practices. In this role, you will be supporting the delivery of high-quality clinical trial activities across multiple concurrent projects. Working under the general direction of the Trial Operations Coordinator and Project Manager, you will play a key part in maintaining essential trial documentation, coordinating daily administrative functions and ensuring operational activities run smoothly. You will support staff, study sites and collaborative groups through accurate information management, proactive problem-solving and timely coordination of trial-related tasks. This is a hands-on, fast-paced and detail-oriented role where your organisational strengths and initiative will support the effective delivery of impactful clinical research. Why work with us We are committed to creating a culturally safe, supportive and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Key benefits include: generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support and professional development opportunities YURA network tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund Your key responsibilities will be to: Provide high-level administrative and operational support by delivering efficient daily services, coordinating correspondence, and maintaining accurate records and filing systems. Coordinate administrative tasks for meetings including preparing materials, organising venues and catering, arranging teleconferences, and taking minutes. Develop, maintain and track trial documentation, study materials, online content and relevant databases in accordance with SOPs and project requirements. Support ethics and regulatory processes by preparing, distributing and tracking submissions, safety data and study documentation. Process site payments and invoices, and maintain up-to-date tracking tools for recruitment, trial supplies, study data and site contacts. Contribute to continuous improvement by identifying administrative issues, proposing solutions, and undertake training to maintain International Council for Harmonisation (ICH) Good Clinical Practice guidelines (GCP) knowledge. About you Bachelor’s degree in Public Health or a relevant field. Demonstrated experience in high-volume administrative environments, ideally within health or clinical trial settings, with strong attention to detail and accuracy. Well-developed communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work independently under general direction and collaboratively within diverse teams. Strong problem-solving skills with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, use initiative and exercise sound judgement in resolving issues. Working knowledge of clinical trials processes, including ICH GCP guidelines, protocol requirements, medical terminology, and basic clinical trials administration. Proficiency with database systems, content management systems and the Microsoft Office suite, including managing electronic documentation and spreadsheets. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   This is a targeted opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer--Education-_0153391-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Education)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nT1-4 Special Skills Placement for the Australasian College of Emergency Medicine (ACEM) with an opportunity to work and teach in the Master of Medicine Located at The University of Sydney School of Medicine, Camperdown Campus - Start Date Feb 2027 Part time (0.5 FTE) position, 6 months fixed term with a base salary of $132,533 p.a. pro rata, plus leave loading and a generous employer’s contribution to superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney Medical School is the largest provider of vocational medical education in Australasia and is at the forefront of online learning for clinicians. The Master of Medicine (Critical Care) was established in 2015 and has a significant enrolment of students and alumni. There are a broad range of study areas available including the basic sciences. You will be supported by a cohort of leading clinicians, academics and medical educators from retrieval and emergency medicine, anesthetics, and intensive care medicine. Since 1856 the Sydney Medical School has been dedicated to developing caring, clinically outstanding, research-capable and globally aware graduates who have the capabilities to become leaders in medicine, public health, and research. We advocate for and take a leadership role in contributing to high-quality healthcare and wellbeing. Our staff play a major role in the provision of healthcare in NSW and in several countries in our region. The Lecturers will assist in the development of learning materials and assessment items, contribute to unit of study teaching activities (mainly online with on-campus workshops in some units). This includes facilitating and moderating online discussion forums and webinars, monitoring student progress and providing feedback and remediation where needed, as well as marking assignments and examination papers. You will contribute to ongoing quality improvement and educational research activity in relation to the Critical Care program. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake teaching in the discipline of critical care medicine, and apply scholarly activity to inform teaching and learning practice in the Master of Medicine (Critical Care Medicine) program engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the University’s expectations and standards suggest, implement, and evaluate improvements and innovations in teaching and learning, where appropriate, under the guidance of more senior academic staff develop an understanding of best practice in teaching and learning within the discipline including engagement with relevant scholarly literature. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Lecturers who have: T1-4 trainee in the Australasian College for Emergency Medicine FACEM registrar training program are a medical graduate (MD, MB BS or equivalent), and eligible for registration with the Medical Board of Australia experience in conducting original research and/or engaging in scholarly activity experience in teaching and learning, as evidenced by participation in team teaching and teaching in a variety of settings demonstrated understanding of the subject-matter within the discipline a developing knowledge and understanding of the key issues in the higher education sector. This is a part-time position at 0.5 FTE for 6 months which is equivalent to 2.5 days per week, and work hours are flexible. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 14 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Modern-Greek-and-Byzantine-Studies_0154355-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Modern Greek and Byzantine Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing An exciting opportunity to conduct research and deliver teaching in the Discipline of Modern Greek and the School of Languages and Cultures Academic Level C Base Salary $156,103- $179,998 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This Senior Lecturer position is open to a candidate capable of providing high-quality teaching, unit development and coordination and research in the Department of Modern Greek and Byzantine studies. The candidate will be required to teach a range of topics in the field of Modern Greek literary, social, political and cultural history, supervise and where necessary participate in the teaching of language units at all levels (beginners, intermediate and advanced); and take an active role in supervision of Honors, Masters and PhD students. To be eligible for the position, applicants are required to hold a PhD in Greek literature, culture and/or history and have experience in teaching and research in higher education institutions. Applicants should have a strong research record of academic publications in Greek literature, culture and/or history and be prepared to take on organisational and administrative roles within the Discipline of Modern Greek and Byzantine Studies and the School of Languages and Cultures. Your key responsibilities will be to: Design, coordinate and teach a range of topics in the field of Modern Greek and Byzantine literary and cultural history; Supervise and participate in the teaching of language units at all levels (beginners, intermediate and advanced); Fulfil the necessary administrative and leadership tasks to ensure the smooth running of the Discipline; Contribute to collaborative teaching at Discipline and School level and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment; Supervise honours and postgraduate research students in Modern Greek and Byzantine Studies; maintain a research profile compatible with the Discipline, School and Faculty research programmes through publication, research grant application and the organization of international conferences and postgraduate or public seminars; Engage in community outreach activities of the Discipline and the School and work with the Greek community in fundraising and the promotion of Greek Studies through lectures and other cultural activities in conjunction with community associations and institutions; Participate in governance and the academic community at School and Faculty levels. About you To be considered for this role, please address the following in your Cover Letter Demonstrate your ability to teach across a broad range of topics in the field of Modern Greek and Byzantine literature, history and culture; and language units of all levels (beginners, intermediate and advanced) Demonstrate the impact of your current research and provide a short outline of your future research aspirations Demonstrate your leadership experience and abilities with regard to the key substantive requirements of the position Demonstrate your abilities as an innovative and creative thinker who can strengthen and expand the program by developing relationships with key stakeholders in Modern Greek Studies. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rae Hao, Recruitment Operations by email to rae.hao@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Technical-Officer_0154398-1": {
      "title": "Senior Technical Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\n4 – Year full time, fixed term opportunity Opportunity to support world-leading single-cell proteomics research infrastructure Base Salary $117,133 - $127,610 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity Sydney Mass Spectrometry (SydneyMS) is a core research facility within the DVC Research Portfolio, providing a suite of over 20 state-of-the-art mass spectrometers. SydneyMS supports research with global impact in the areas of metabolomics, lipidomics, proteomics, and mass spectrometry imaging. The facility provides best in class services to internal and external clients to the University, including industry, and across a wide range of disciplines in medical and life sciences. As the Senior Technical Officer , you will play a key role in supporting the ACRF Single-Cell Cancer Proteomics Laboratory.  The laboratory has been equipped with a comprehensive set of equipment for single cell analysis including a LMD7 microscope, a cellenONE single cell sorter, and an Orbitrap Astral Zoom mass spectrometer.  You will be providing specialist technical expertise, operational oversight and advanced scientific support across these cutting-edge single-cell proteomics platforms. Working within a collaborative research environment, you will oversee laboratory operations, undertake experimental work, support research projects, train users and researchers, and maintain sophisticated instrumentation. You will contribute to the delivery of high-quality research outcomes while ensuring laboratory activities are conducted safely, efficiently and in accordance with University requirements. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide expert technical advice and undertake experiments in collaboration with researchers and technical staff oversee the operation, optimisation and maintenance of laboratory instrumentation and equipment support single-cell proteomics and related research workflows across a multi-user facility train researchers, students, staff and visitors in the safe and effective use of laboratory equipment and techniques perform risk assessments and develop safe work and maintenance procedures for laboratory operations manage technical information requests, quotations and support for fee-for-service and contract research activities analyse and interpret experimental data and contribute to scientific reporting and technical documentation coach and mentor users while contributing to a collaborative and high-performing laboratory environment ensure compliance with work health and safety requirements and maintain a clean, safe laboratory environment About you Required: demonstrated experience supporting or undertaking LCMS research within proteomics, metabolomics, lipidomics proven experience operating, troubleshooting, optimising and maintaining mass spectrometry and/or liquid chromatography instrumentation, particularly nanoflow LCs and orbitrap mass spectrometers within life or medical sciences research settings experience working within a multi-user laboratory environment, including vendor liaison and equipment coordination demonstrated ability to deliver specialised technical training and support to other researchers, users and staff strong analytical, problem-solving and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to build effective stakeholder relationships a bachelor's degree in a relevant scientific discipline, or an equivalent combination of training and experience Desired: demonstrated experience supporting or undertaking LCMS research within proteomics, metabolomics, lipidomics, for single-cell and/or spatial omics analysis knowledge and practical experience in optical light microscopy, especially in the area of laser capture microdissection knowledge and practical experience in cell sorting and flow cytometry knowledge and practical experience in data analysis and data visualisation, especially in the use of R a PhD in biomedical research that was heavily based on mass spectrometry-based Omics analysis Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 05 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer--Teaching-and-Research-_0155935-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Pharmacy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\n` 2 full-time, continuing positions available at Camperdown Campus Fantastic opportunity to make a significant contribution to the teaching and research endeavours of the Sydney Pharmacy School Base Salary from $132,533 + 17 % superannuation About the opportunity   The Sydney Pharmacy School is internationally recognised for excellence in pharmacy education and research, driving innovation across medication management, pharmaceutical sciences, patient care, and health policy. Our academics and researchers undertake world-class research spanning medicinal chemistry, drug delivery, cellular and systems pharmacology, and health services research, contributing to improved health outcomes from discovery through to patient care and policy.   We are seeking to appoint two Lecturers in Pharmacy (Level B) to join our collegial and high-performing academic community during an exciting period of growth and transformation. In this role, you will contribute to the delivery of outstanding education through teaching and supporting students across pharmacy and pharmacology disciplines, while developing your own program of impactful research.   As part of the Sydney Pharmacy School, you will collaborate with leading academics and researchers across the Faculty of Medicine and Health, the broader health sector, and the University’s multidisciplinary research initiatives. This continuing position offers a balanced academic workload of 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service, providing an opportunity to make significant contributions to teaching and learning, engage in educational leadership, and build a strong research and innovation portfolio within a supportive and multidisciplinary environment.   Key Responsibilities:   Contribute to the delivery of outstanding education through high-quality teaching, learning, assessment and student support across pharmacy, pharmacology and related disciplines Lead and contribute to curriculum design, review and educational innovation, ensuring contemporary, engaging and evidence-based learning experiences that support student success Develop and maintain an active research program that contributes to the School's research excellence, profile and impact Supervise and mentor higher degree by research students, fostering a supportive and high-performing research environment Establish and strengthen collaborations with academic, industry, healthcare and community partners to enhance research quality, translation and impact. Contribute to educational leadership activities within the School and Faculty, supporting continuous improvement in teaching and learning Undertake service and administrative responsibilities that support the effective operation of the School, Faculty and University Contribute positively to a collaborative and inclusive academic culture that values diversity, innovation, excellence and mutual respect Undertake other duties commensurate with the level and responsibilities of the position.   About You A PhD in Pharmacy, Pharmacology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, or a closely related discipline Demonstrated expertise in pharmacy, pharmacology or pharmaceutical sciences, with the ability to contribute to teaching and research across relevant areas of the Sydney Pharmacy School An emerging or established research track record, evidenced by high-quality scholarly outputs and a clear potential to develop a sustained program of research Experience in teaching and learning within higher education, including curriculum development, unit coordination, assessment design and the delivery of engaging, student-centred learning experiences Demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating innovative teaching and learning initiatives that enhance student outcomes Experience in supervising, mentoring and supporting undergraduate, postgraduate and/or higher degree by research students The ability to build productive collaborations and contribute to multidisciplinary research, teaching and engagement activities An understanding of current and emerging issues within the discipline and the higher education sector, with the capacity to contribute to educational and research priorities A commitment to fostering inclusive, equitable and respectful learning and working environments that support the success of students and colleagues from diverse backgrounds Excellent interpersonal, communication and organisational skills, with the ability to contribute positively to a collaborative academic community. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi , Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
