    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Implementation-Lead---Complaints-Technology-Solution_0150899-1": {
      "title": "Implementation Lead - Complaints Technology Solution",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\n2 x Full time, fixed term opportunities until 31 December 2027 Help Shape the Future of Complaints Management at the University HE0 10: $162,528 plus 17% Superannuation About the opportunity   The Implementation Lead - Complaints Technology Solution plays a pivotal role in the delivery of the University's new enterprise complaints management platform. Acting as a key link between project teams, business stakeholders and technical specialists, you will help translate strategic objectives into practical processes, workflows and operational outcomes. While the role sits within a major transformation program, it is distinct from a traditional Project Manager or Change Manager position. Instead, you'll work across multiple project and operational workstreams, connecting stakeholders, subject matter experts and technical teams to help translate strategic objectives into practical processes, documentation, governance and implementation outcomes. Working closely with vendors, Digital Sydney, ICT teams and business stakeholders, you'll coordinate implementation activities across multiple workstreams, support stakeholder engagement, facilitate process design, validate data and documentation, and help prepare the University for successful adoption of the new solution. Key responsibilities Coordinate implementation activities across business, technology and operational workstreams to support the successful delivery of the enterprise complaints management solution. Partner with stakeholders and subject matter experts to design, document and improve complaint management processes and workflows. Act as a key point of contact between project teams, ICT, vendors and business units, facilitating collaboration and issue resolution. Support governance, compliance, documentation, reporting, data validation and risk management activities throughout implementation. Develop and manage workstream-level implementation plans, priorities and deliverables, ensuring alignment with broader project objectives. Contribute to change readiness activities, including stakeholder engagement, testing, training support and adoption planning. About you We're seeking adaptable and collaborative professionals who enjoy bringing people, processes and technology together to deliver outcomes. You'll be comfortable navigating ambiguity, building relationships across diverse stakeholder groups and working in environments undergoing significant change. Experience working within complaints management, case management, student conduct, workplace relations, investigations, research integrity, regulatory compliance or similar operational environments. Demonstrated ability to coordinate complex initiatives across multiple stakeholder groups in a large or highly regulated organisation. Experience in higher education, student services, workplace relations, research integrity, misconduct, internal audit or similar governance-focused environments will be highly regarded. Strong process analysis and process design capability, with experience documenting workflows and identifying improvement opportunities. Excellent stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships and influence outcomes without direct authority. An agile and solutions-focused mindset, with the ability to adapt to changing priorities and operate effectively in ambiguous environments. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Food-and-Beverage-Manager_0155455-1": {
      "title": "Food and Beverage Manager - Seymour Centre",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Lead dynamic food and beverage operations at the heart of live performance and entertainment $85,000 - $92,000 plus 12% Superannuation About the opportunity   The Food and Beverage Manager plays a key role in delivering exceptional hospitality experiences at the Seymour Centre, leading all food and beverage operations across bars, catering services and event hospitality. This hands-on leadership role is responsible for ensuring outstanding customer service, driving commercial performance and creating a welcoming and memorable experience for patrons attending performances, events and functions. Working closely with operational leaders, you will oversee supplier partnerships, stock management, staffing, financial performance and compliance activities while identifying opportunities to enhance service delivery and revenue growth. You will negotiate supplier agreements, manage budgets and ensure food and beverage offerings remain competitive, profitable and aligned with industry best practice. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced hospitality professional who thrives in a fast-paced events environment. Combining strategic oversight with day-to-day operational leadership, you will lead and develop a high-performing team while contributing to the ongoing success and reputation of one of Sydney’s leading performing arts venues. This is a full-time position working 38 hours per week. Given the nature of the performing arts industry, evening and weekend work will be required to support performances, opening nights and special events. Key responsibilities Lead the day-to-day operation of food and beverage outlets, ensuring exceptional service standards and operational efficiency. Manage supplier relationships, negotiate contracts and maintain attractive, high-quality food and beverage offerings. Recruit, roster, coach and develop hospitality staff to build a skilled, engaged and customer-focused team. Oversee budgets, stock control, cash management, reconciliations and pricing strategies to drive financial performance. Manage catering services for performances, functions and events from enquiry through to successful delivery. Ensure compliance with WHS and RSA requirements while supporting continuous improvement initiatives across hospitality operations. About you Demonstrated experience managing food and beverage, hospitality, catering or event operations, ideally within arts, entertainment, live performance, venue or customer-facing environments. Proven ability to lead, motivate and develop teams while maintaining high standards of customer service. Strong financial and commercial acumen, including budget management, forecasting and profitability improvement. Excellent stakeholder engagement and negotiation skills, with the ability to build effective supplier and client relationships. Experience using POS systems, managing stock control processes and overseeing cash handling and reconciliation activities. A proactive and adaptable approach, combined with a genuine passion for hospitality, live events and creating exceptional customer experiences. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
