    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Executive-Assistant-to-the-Head-of-School-and-Dean_0155272-2": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the Head of School and Dean",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing A unique opportunity to work at the heart of the University of Sydney Law School, providing trusted, high level support to the Head of School and Dean in a dynamic, high impact role HEO 7 Base Salary $-117,133 - $127,610 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity Join one of Australia's most respected law schools. The University of Sydney Law School combines outstanding teaching, influential research, and strong industry connections to prepare students for successful and meaningful legal careers. Committed to shaping the next generation of legal professionals, the School offers a dynamic, intellectually rigorous learning environment that inspires critical thinking, innovation, and leadership. As the first point of contact on behalf of the Head of School and Dean, you will play a critical role in managing complex workflows, prioritising correspondence, and ensuring the timely resolution of enquiries and matters of significance. Leveraging your strong relationship management and communication skills, you will collaborate effectively with senior stakeholders across the University and external industry partners to support the School's strategic objectives and deliver service excellence. This pivotal role sits at the very heart of the Law School, offering a unique opportunity to work closely with senior leadership and contribute to the effective operation of the Dean's Office and, more broadly, the Dean’s Unit. The role therefore requires an on-campus presence three days per week at a minimum and as required by the Head of School and Dean and Deputy Dean. What you will love about this role As the Head of School and Dean's trusted Executive Assistant, you'll be at the heart of a vibrant and intellectually engaging environment, working alongside respected academic and professional leaders. You'll have the opportunity to influence how the Dean's Office operates, engage with a broad network of stakeholders, and contribute to the success of a globally recognised institution. This role offers a rewarding combination of strategic exposure, operational variety, and meaningful impact. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide high-level, confidential executive support to the Dean and Deputy Dean, including proactive diary management, coordination of meetings, travel, expenses, and preparation of briefings and correspondence act as the first point of contact on behalf of the Dean and Deputy Dean, managing complex enquiries, coordinating executive priorities, and ensuring the efficient operation of the Dean's Office support the effective operation of Sydney Law School's leadership through the coordination of governance and committee activities, including meeting preparation, agendas, minutes, action tracking, and follow-up activities coordinate strategic initiatives, leadership priorities and executive projects, ensuring actions, deadlines and deliverables are effectively managed maintain effective document management, governance and information systems, including SharePoint and collaboration platforms build strong relationships with senior stakeholders across the University and external partners, while ensuring compliance with University policies and contributing to continuous improvement initiatives manage travel, expense and procurement processes in accordance with University policies and financial requirements. About you Relevant tertiary qualification and/or an equivalent combination of education, training and significant relevant professional experience Demonstrated experience providing high-level executive and administrative support in a complex organisation, preferably within higher education or a similarly large and multifaceted environment Exceptional organisational, time management and prioritisation skills, with the ability to manage competing demands, coordinate multiple activities simultaneously and consistently meet deadlines Highly developed written communication skills, including the ability to prepare professional correspondence, briefing papers, reports and other documentation for senior leaders Advanced proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and digital collaboration tools, with extensive experience in complex diary management, meeting coordination and information management systems Strong interpersonal and stakeholder management skills, with the ability to build effective relationships, handle sensitive enquiries, negotiate outcomes and provide a high standard of customer service Proven ability to exercise sound judgement, discretion and initiative when managing confidential matters, navigating governance processes and supporting senior executive decision-making. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. You must be an Australian citizen or a permanent resident to apply. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rae Hao, Recruitment Operations by email to rae.hao@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 10 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Development-Associate--Sydney-Conservatorium-of-Music-_0156264-2": {
      "title": "Development Associate (Sydney Conservatorium of Music)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nA great opportunity if you're passionate about music and the performing arts to step into the role and make an impact. The University of Sydney has a new opportunity covering a secondment for 12 months for a self-motivated and passionate fundraiser to join the Advancement Portfolio’s growing Arts and Culture team as Development Associate. The role will have a focus on procuring and managing major gift opportunities at the prestigious Sydney Conservatorium of Music through donor cultivation, solicitation, stewardship and managing donor and internal stakeholder relations to set up opportunities to their greatest success. Philanthropy requires thought, passion and action. It involves creating social impact and caring enough about the needs of others to help. Generosity worldwide has initiated groundbreaking research, provided millions of educational opportunities and built stronger communities. This is the power of giving. The Sydney Conservatorium of Music, or ‘the Con’ as it is affectionately known, provides a distinctive and innovative environment for musicians, composers and music scholars to learn, create and perform, preparing students to thrive in today’s evolving music industry. The Con awards over $1.5 million in scholarships every year thanks to its proud community of donors. As it continues to reimagine the future for music education, the Con partners with philanthropists and the University to realise a range of groundbreaking programs, development initiatives and game-changing research. From the concert hall to the big screen, the orchestra pit to the Xbox or from the opera and music theatre to the fabric of communities, the demand for our art and impact of music is greater than ever. In this role you will develop relationships with high net worth donors, connecting their passions with work that would otherwise not be undertaken, or education and outreach opportunities to support the next generation of leaders and thinkers. You will build strong internal relationships with academic leaders to understand how their critical work can be aligned with donor passions. You would be a part of lifting the profile of philanthropy across the institution. About you The University values trust, accountability and respect and we value the spirit of collaboration, achieving more together. As such, we seek to recruit talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Development Associate, Sydney Conservatorium of Music , who: Is a natural relationship builder – you enjoy interacting with people one-on-one and fostering relationships with people at different levels and from different cultures has excellent communication skills, both listening and written in order to develop compelling proposals or cases for support that align to a prospective donor’s interests has highly developed emotional intelligence – you are self-aware with exceptional interpersonal skills and an active listener with a proactive work ethic   is a high-achiever and naturally driven by success both professionally and personally has prior experience either in fundraising, development, sales or relationship management – please note, prior experience is not essential . If you have transferrable skills or experiences from others field and are a motivated, fast learner , we’d love to hear from you   is passionate about the performing arts or music in its many forms and can demonstrate this in a cover letter, along with why you would be a suitable strong candidate for this role About us The Advancement Portfolio team at the University of Sydney is one of the most successful fundraising programs in Australian higher education. Having achieved the nation’s first $1BN fundraising campaign and onto their second campaign, the team is not only the finest performing, its success facilitates the largest real-world impact that philanthropic giving makes possible. Since its inception 175 years ago, the University of Sydney has led to improve the world around us. We believe in education for all and that effective leadership makes lives better. These same values are reflected in our approach to philanthropy and underpin our long-term strategy for excellence and growth. We’re Australia's first university and have an outstanding global reputation for academic and research excellence. Across our campuses, we employ over 7600 academic and non-academic staff who support over 60,000 students. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. For a continuing position, you must be an Australian or New Zealand citizen or an Australian Permanent Resident. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  In your cover letter, please ensure you respond to our key bullet-points under our ‘About you’ section. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Sarah Scott, Recruitment Partner by email to sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au Applications Close Saturday 12 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
