    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Director--Institute-of-Transport-and-Logistics-Studies_0156399-2": {
      "title": "Director, Institute of Transport and Logistics Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nShape the future direction of one of the world’s premier transport and logistics research institutes, delivering meaningful impact across academia, industry, government and the broader community. The successful candidate will be appointed to a continuing substantive level E (Professorial) role in the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chains and seconded for five years to the Director of ITLS role on an FTE basis commensurate with the directorship duties. Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation. About the opportunity The Institute of Transport and Logistics Studies (ITLS) is one of the world's leading institutes for transport, logistics and supply chain research, education and policy engagement. Ranked #1 in Australia and #8 globally for Transportation Science and Technology (Shanghai Ranking 2025), ITLS has built an international reputation for research excellence, policy influence and industry engagement over almost four decades. With strong partnerships across government, industry and international organisations, and a track record of shaping transport, infrastructure and logistics policy and practice, ITLS offers a rare opportunity to lead a globally recognised institute with significant impact beyond academia. As Director, ITLS, you will provide strategic, academic and operational leadership for this globally recognised research institute within the University of Sydney Business School.  You will advance research excellence, education, industry and government engagement, and organisational performance, positioning ITLS as a leading source of evidence and expertise across transport, infrastructure, logistics and supply chain management. In this role, you will: provide strategic, academic and operational leadership for ITLS, setting its direction and aligning its priorities with those of the Business School and University foster research excellence by supporting high-impact research, external funding, research translation and interdisciplinary collaboration across transport, infrastructure, logistics and supply chain management build and strengthen strategic partnerships with government, industry, professional bodies, alumni and international collaborators to extend the Institute’s impact, profile and reputation coordinate and contribute to high-quality teaching and learning, including the successful supervision of higher degree research students oversee the Institute’s planning, governance, compliance and continuous improvement, ensuring effective resource management, reporting and delivery of strategic priorities support the development and delivery of ITLS’s specialist teaching and executive education offerings, research training and workforce capability development foster an inclusive culture of innovation, collaboration and academic excellence manage the Institute’s financial, human and physical resources to support its sustainability and organisational effectiveness represent and promote ITLS across academic, industry, government and community forums, positioning it as a trusted source of impartial, evidence-based research champion work addressing emerging challenges in sustainable transport, mobility, infrastructure, logistics and resilient supply chains, with meaningful impact on policy, practice and society. About you (skills, capabilities and experience)  You will be an accomplished academic leader with a PhD in a field relevant to ITLS, an established national and international research reputation, and the capacity to lead a high-performing institute. You will bring: deep knowledge of the strategic, policy, industry and academic issues affecting transport, infrastructure, logistics or supply chain management a distinguished record of research and teaching, together with the capacity to provide outstanding academic leadership and articulate a clear strategic direction experience developing and leading a high-performing academic centre, research group or comparable knowledge-intensive organisation established national and international networks across academia, industry, government and the professions a strong record of engagement with government, industry and external stakeholders an understanding of how research can inform policy, regulation, industry practice and public value experience managing operational priorities, governance, budgets, reporting and performance in a complex academic environment strong communication, negotiation and stakeholder management skills experience supporting interdisciplinary collaboration across academic disciplines, professional teams and external partners a commitment to sustainability, equity and public value in transport, logistics, infrastructure and supply chain systems demonstrated experience delivering high-quality teaching and learning, including higher degree research supervision. Key benefits  the opportunity to work within one of Australia’s leading universities a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund generous relocation allowance if moving from interstate or overseas  access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have the right to work in Australia before commencing employment. Visa sponsorship may be available for the successful candidate if required. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be appointed into a continuing substantive level E (Professorial) role in the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chains and seconded for five years to the Director of ITLS role on an FTE basis commensurate with the directorship duties. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  To meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications, including a CV and cover letter of no more than four pages addressing the requirements of the role, should be submitted through the Odgers website. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description at the bottom for further details. The preferred method of application is online at https://www.odgers.com/en-au/opportunities/ . All applications will receive an automated response. Should you be unable to submit your application online, please email it to: Sophie.Malone@odgers.com For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. The University has engaged executive search firm Odgers to assist with this appointment who will support the Selection Committee to identify the widest possible field of qualified candidates and to assess candidates against the requirements for the role. For a confidential conversation, please contact: Sophie Malone on Sophie.Malone@odgers.com or +61 2 8905 3703 Applications close at 11.59pm AEDT on Wednesday 14 October 2026. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Click to view the Candidate Information Pack for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 14 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_0156332-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull Time 12-month fixed term role for a dedicated Research Fellow Opportunity to join the Brain and Mind Centre to study the role of the immune system in mental health problems among young people Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Brain and Mind Centre’s Youth Mental Health and Technology team conduct clinical research across several streams that explore biological and behavioural factors influencing the onset, course, and response to treatment in young people with emerging mental disorders. Extensive collaborations with clinicians, data scientists, geneticists, bioinformaticians, health service providers, and philanthropic and research organisations enable us to quickly and effectively translate our research findings into health system innovations, facilitating continuous improvements to mental health services for the benefit of young people in Australia. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake analysis of clinical, biological, and behavioural data and work as part of a collaborative team working at the intersection of the immune system and mental health to guide better assessment, classification, and intervention in young people with emerging mental disorders assist with the design and high-level coordination of new study protocols collecting clinical, biological, and behavioural data as part of immune-related mental health research projects assist with the preparation of project reports, conference presentations, and journal manuscripts (including data management/preparation, statistical analysis, visualisation, and writing) assist with the preparation of grant and fellowship applications (institutional, national, and/or international schemes). About you a PhD in an academic field relevant to immune science or other higher professional qualifications in related fields (e.g., neuroscience) experience in quantitative methods relevant to immune science experience in clinical research with strong data analytic skills with an interest in training in the application of analytic techniques to longitudinal or large mental health data sets or biobanks a strong applied research orientation with a proven ability to build successful working relationships with a variety of stakeholders (e.g., clinicians, service providers), both internal and external strong analytical skills and a methodical approach to data management and analysis strong interpersonal and communication skills. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 . EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion, and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger, Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 25 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Veterinary-Nurse_0153850-3": {
      "title": "Veterinary Nurse",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing* opportunity.  Located on the Camden Campus at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camden (UVTHC) Exciting opportunity to provide best practice veterinary care in small animal general practice Base Salary HEO 3 (38 hpw), $82,388 - $91,488 p.a. + 17% superannuation *Full Time Funding contingent continuing position About the opportunity Join one of Australia’s leading veterinary teaching hospitals and take the next step in your veterinary nursing career with a full-time continuing* opportunity at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camden (UVTHC). This is a unique chance to work in a collaborative, gold-standard small animal practice where no two days are the same, gaining exposure to a diverse caseload.. Working alongside highly experienced veterinarians and final-year veterinary interns, you’ll play a vital role in delivering exceptional patient care while contributing to the development of the next generation of veterinary professionals. With a competitive salary plus 17% superannuation, a supportive team environment, and the opportunity to broaden your clinical skills in a respected university setting, this role offers the perfect blend of hands-on nursing, professional growth and meaningful impact. Your key responsibilities will be to:  assist clinicians and veterinary interns with treatments, prepare and assist with surgeries and clinical procedures face-to-face enquiries, basic triage, and admission and discharge of patients clean and maintain clinical  spaces sterilise instruments and equipment, prepare and pack surgical kits maintain stock including ordering of consumables and procurement of supplies, and control of stock quantities.  The small animal service at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camden (UVTHC) operates in: a general small animal veterinary practice wide range of all medical and surgical problems second opinion cases. To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here About you a Veterinary Nursing Certificate IV and/or advanced veterinary nursing experience in a small animal veterinary hospital environment knowledge of a large range of veterinary, surgical, and medical procedures demonstrated ability to cope with emergencies and stressful situations demonstrated ability to work independently and in a team experience working in a customer service environment with demonstrated high levels of care for patients and clients good attention to detail a willingness and ability to work rostered shifts, Monday to Friday the ability to communicate with veterinary professionals and academic staff demonstrated ability to lift animals up to 16kg and heavier animals with assistance (mechanical and/or team lifting), adhering to the University’s Heavy Lifting Policy competence and experience in best practice veterinary anaesthesia experience working in a veterinary teaching hospital environment desirable a valid driver’s licence vaccination for Q Fever or willingness to be vaccinated.   The key benefits: opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund access to the employee assistance program access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres industry-leading leave provisions, including generous paid parental leave entitlements (pending eligibility up to 36 weeks) gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Technical-Officer---School-of-Medical-Sciences_0156408-1": {
      "title": "Senior Technical Officer - School of Medical Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position      Exciting opportunity to be a Senior Technical Officer within the School of Medical Sciences Base Salary starting at $131,108 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Medical Sciences (SOMS) is seeking a Senior Technical Officer to provide specialist technical, operational and infrastructure support to the Biomanufacturing Incubator, enabling high-quality research activity and supporting the delivery of innovative biomanufacturing projects. This laboratory-based role will oversee the operation and maintenance of specialised biomanufacturing equipment, including 3D bioprinters and associated instrumentation, while providing technical guidance and support to researchers using the facility. The Senior Technical Officer will contribute to the establishment and optimisation of facility workflows, support experimental activities, coordinate access to shared resources and ensure equipment, processes and laboratory operations meet safety, quality and operational requirements. Working collaboratively with researchers, students, academic partners, industry stakeholders and clinical collaborators, the role will support the growth of the Biomanufacturing Incubator through user training, continuous improvement initiatives and contributions to infrastructure development, including grant collaborations and equipment acquisition activities. Your key responsibilities will be to: maintain, operate and monitor specialised biomanufacturing equipment, including 3D bioprinters and associated laboratory instrumentation support the design and optimisation of biomanufacturing laboratory infrastructure and facility workflows coordinate equipment servicing, calibration, preventative maintenance and troubleshooting to ensure reliable and safe operation provide hands-on technical support to researchers during experimental setup, equipment use and workflow execution assist with method optimisation, equipment configuration and routine troubleshooting during research activities prepare laboratory materials, consumables and facility resources required for biomanufacturing projects deliver onboarding and training sessions for new facility users, including laboratory orientation, safety requirements and operational procedures support laboratory operations by implementing and maintaining standard operating procedures, safety requirements and quality processes monitor laboratory conditions, consumable inventory and operational workflows to maintain an efficient research environment coordinate equipment bookings, facility access and shared resource allocation to optimise facility utilisation communicate operational updates, maintenance requirements and scheduling changes to researchers and project teams identify opportunities to improve facility workflows, equipment performance and user experience support the implementation of new technologies, processes and equipment within the Biomanufacturing Incubator contribute to infrastructure acquisition activities, including supporting grant applications and collaborations with academic, industry and clinical partners undertake other duties and contribute to projects appropriate within the classification level as required. About you postgraduate qualifications in a relevant field or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience certified to work in a laboratory environment significant experience working in a laboratory environment, with demonstrated experience supporting research activities significant experience preparing and conducting experiments using established research methodologies experience operating, maintaining and troubleshooting laboratory equipment and instrumentation experience implementing laboratory safety requirements and maintaining compliance with relevant procedures experience using databases and/or statistical analysis programs to support research activities strong analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to resolve technical issues and provide practical solutions excellent organisational and planning skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and support multiple research projects strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build effective relationships with researchers, students and stakeholders ability to work independently while contributing effectively as part of a collaborative research environment. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 23 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Research-Fellow-School-of-Public-Health_0156841-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow-School of Public Health",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.40 FTE) fixed term 12 months at Camperdown Senior Research Fellow role supporting Base Salary $ 162,347 (pro rata 0.40FTE) + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity Founded in 1930, the Sydney School of Public Health is Australia’s first and leading school in public health, dedicated to addressing the world's most pressing health challenges. Each day, our researchers and graduates are tackling global health issues and striving to improve equity in communities both locally and around the world. We are seeking a Senior Research Fellow who will lead a research program on misleading medical marketing of social media and AI, co-designing, evaluating using mixed method studies and implementing evidence-based solutions to address this public health issue. The role will support Dr Brooke Nickel and her team with the NHMRC funded Synergy grant. The Synergy Grant aims \"to carry out world-leading cross-disciplinary research integrating health literacy with health AI technology development and implementation\" . This ambitious program will support critical efforts to bridge the AI health literacy gap in Australia and globally, ensuring AI-driven healthcare is understandable, accessible and usable for everyone, advancing equitable health outcomes both nationally and internationally. Your key responsibilities will be to: build and maintain a reputation for academic excellence through independent and innovative contributions to research, expanding knowledge and practice in the discipline, with a particular focus on advancing the objectives of the NHMRC Synergy Grant investigating health literacy, social media and AI technologies and misleading medical marketing. disseminate research knowledge and facilitate knowledge transfer to drive impact, including translating findings into evidence-based solutions that improve the accessibility, understanding and safe use of social media and AI-driven healthcare. maintain a research profile, including publication in academic or professional journals and engagement in keynote addresses, showcasing leadership in health literacy, digital health and low value care research. lead successful competitive research grants and/or generate other research income by exploring diverse funding opportunities, building on the research program and collaborative networks established through the NHMRC Synergy Grant. maintain quality, standards and excellence in research activities and output, ensuring the successful delivery of high-quality, impactful research outcomes across the Synergy Grant program. provide mentoring and guidance to lower-level academics, research staff and higher degree research students involved in the Sydney Health Literacy Lab and Wiser Healthcare research collaboration. make a significant and holistic contribution to the research, administrative and governance activities of the School or Faculty, including participating in committees and academic boards, and contributing to the effective management and reporting requirements of the Synergy Grant. contribute to a positive workplace culture in which diversity of thought, contribution, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes are valued and enabled, fostering productive interdisciplinary and international collaborations across the Synergy Grant partnership and the Wiser Healthcare research collaboration. understand and contribute to furthering the University and Faculty/School strategic agenda, including strengthening the University's leadership in health literacy, digital health (social media and AI) and public health research. engage in community and alumni outreach, contribute expertise to public debates, and play a significant role in scholarly, research, or professional activities relevant to area of expertise, supporting the Synergy Grant's goal of bridging the digital health literacy gap in Australia and globally. establish partnerships, collaborations and engage in translational research for impactful outcomes in research, development, commercialisation, and policy improvement, working closely with investigators from three countries and eight institutions led by the Sydney Health Literacy Lab to advance equitable health outcomes through AI-driven healthcare. About you a PhD in public health, health communication, health services research or a closely related discipline a strong track record of high-quality research outputs, including publications in internationally recognised peer-reviewed journals and evidence of research impact experience leading and managing complex research projects and multidisciplinary, cross-institutional collaborations, ideally involving national and international partners expertise in mixed methods research, evaluation approaches and the co-design of interventions to improve health outcomes demonstrated success in obtaining competitive research funding or making substantial contributions to successful grant applications and funded research programs experience supervising and mentoring research staff, early career researchers and higher degree research students strong stakeholder engagement skills, with experience working across academic, government, community, healthcare and/or policy sectors to deliver research translation and impact excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to publish in high-impact journals, present at national and international conferences, and translate research findings for diverse audiences demonstrated commitment to research excellence, collaboration and positive societal impact experience in health literacy, digital health, social media research, artificial intelligence, health communication, misinformation or implementation research the ability to build strategic partnerships and contribute to translational research that informs policy, practice and public understanding of health-related issues a demonstrated capacity to contribute to an internationally recognised research program. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 28 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Exam-Supervision-Officer_0157174": {
      "title": "Exam Supervision Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nMultiple positions available – seeking flexible availability from 16th – 28th November 2026 (weekend and evening shifts also available) Paid mandatory training will commence the week beginning 26th October 2026 HE02 Step 1 - $47.65 per hour plus Superannuation About the opportunity The Examination Supervision Officer plays a key role in supporting the delivery of examinations across the University, working closely with the Exams Office within Student Administration Services (SAS). This hands-on position is responsible for facilitating the smooth and efficient conduct of examinations in line with University policies and procedures. In this role, you will primarily assist with the supervision of centrally managed examinations, including BYOD (Bring Your Own Device) assessments and alternative examination arrangements for students requiring adjustments. You will work collaboratively with Exam Office staff to ensure a positive, equitable, and well-organised examination experience for all students. You will provide frontline support to students during formal examination sessions, monitor compliance with examination protocols, and promptly escalate any issues or incidents to the Exams Office as required. This is an excellent opportunity for individuals who are proactive, professional, and committed to delivering exceptional student service in a fast-paced environment. We are seeking multiple Examination Supervision Officers to support with the upcoming examination period, which will run from 16th – 28th November 2026 . This role is ideal for an individual with flexible and full availability over this 2-week period (evening and weekend shifts also available). Please outline your availability across the 16th – 28th November 2026 when submitting your application. If successful, you will also be required to undertake mandatory training components (mix of online and on-site training) from 26th October 2026. Your key responsibilities effective administration of examination rooms, in compliance with existing procedures provide guidance to students regarding exam processes, including those utilising computer programs to conduct examinations supporting students who require adjustments to fully participate in the examination process set up of examination venues, including paperwork and computer equipment, receiving, and checking computer systems to facilitate the efficient delivery of both paper and computer-based/ Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) examinations on campus for the duration of the exam, constantly monitor examination room(s) to ensure students comply with all instructions and examination rules, and to prevent and detect academic misconduct accurately validate the identity of each student and ensure all conditions, including time restrictions, are adhered to by all candidates complete reports for any incidents or suspected misconduct cases in accordance with University policy and procedure support the activities of the portfolio to ensure compliance and service excellence to students and staff. About you strong computer skills and knowledge, including MS Office Suite, to assist students with computer-based examinations and navigating the online examination portal for ‘Bring your Own Device’ examinations keen interest in supporting students requiring examination adjustments capacity to remain observant for long periods, punctual and reliable, calm, and cheerful demeanour accurate and efficient administrative skills with excellent attention to detail the ability to command a room and comfortable addressing a large group of students capability to maintain continuous mobility for long periods of time and carry documentation, including examination papers between venues well-developed interpersonal skills, including the demonstrated ability to work both independently and as part of a team ability to use initiative, exercise sound judgement related to role responsibility Important to note: undergraduate or postgraduate students from the University of Sydney (coursework-based students) are NOT eligible to apply. PHD students, research students or masters by research students are eligible to apply. We will be reviewing applications and contacting candidates upon submission, so please kindly apply at your earliest convenience. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia  You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .     How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Casual Recruitment, by email to casual-recruitment.admin@sydney.edu.au    © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 18 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Sydney/Research-Assistant---Multiple-Sclerosis_0152727-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Multiple Sclerosis",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart-time (17.5 hours per week), 12-month fixed-term opportunity Join a leading neuroimaging research team at the University of Sydney and contribute to groundbreaking multiple sclerosis research while developing your expertise in MRI analysis, Python-based data pipelines, and clinical research coordination Base Salary $89,755 - $100,495 (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity Join the Save Sight Institute at the University of Sydney and play a key role in a major MRFF-funded multiple sclerosis neuroimaging study that is advancing the understanding of disease progression and treatment outcomes. Working within a multidisciplinary research team, you will contribute to the delivery of cutting-edge research through a combination of advanced MRI data processing, neuroimaging workflow coordination, and study administration. We are seeking a highly organised and technically capable Research Assistant to support a large MRFF-funded multiple sclerosis imaging study based at the University of Sydney. The role combines advanced neuroimaging data management and MRI pipeline execution with day-to-day study coordination responsibilities. The successful candidate will work with large-scale longitudinal clinical and imaging datasets derived from the MSBase Registry and MSBase Imaging Repository (MSBIR), including extraction and organisation of treatment and MRI timepoint data, coordination of imaging workflows, and execution of MRI preprocessing pipelines to prepare data for downstream lesion analysis. Responsibilities will include image registration, quality control, pipeline management, coordination of data flow across collaborating sites, and assisting with overall study administration and project coordination. The position requires experience working with MRI analysis pipelines and neuroimaging software (e.g. FSL, ANTs, FreeSurfer, MRtrix or similar), proficiency in Python and command-line scripting for data processing and automation, strong organisational skills, and the ability to work independently within a multidisciplinary MS neuroimaging research team. The role is expected to involve approximately 80% MRI/data processing work and 20% study coordination activities. Your key responsibilities will be to: Coordinate and support the delivery of a large-scale multiple sclerosis neuroimaging research study, ensuring research activities are completed accurately and within agreed timeframes. Manage and process complex longitudinal clinical and MRI datasets, including data extraction, organisation, quality control, and preparation for downstream analysis. Execute and maintain MRI preprocessing and analysis pipelines, supporting image registration, workflow management, and data integrity across collaborating research sites. Write and maintain Python and shell scripts to automate data extraction, cross-reference large longitudinal datasets across registries and clinical records, and generate reproducible outputs (tables, plots, summary statistics) to support pipeline execution and reporting. Collaborate with researchers, clinicians, and external partners to facilitate study operations, coordinate imaging workflows, and contribute to project objectives and outcomes. Prepare and maintain study documentation, reports, ethics and governance records, research outputs, and other project-related materials in accordance with regulatory and institutional requirements. Support the administrative and operational requirements of the research program, including stakeholder communication, financial processing, compliance activities, and continuous improvement initiatives. About you Tertiary qualifications in a relevant field. Demonstrated experience working with MRI analysis pipelines and neuroimaging software such as FSL, ANTs, FreeSurfer, MRtrix, or similar tools, with the ability to manage and process complex imaging datasets. Proficiency in Python (e.g. pandas, numpy, matplotlib) and command-line/terminal environments, with demonstrated ability to write scripts for data wrangling, automation, and generation of graphs and summary reports from large, complex datasets. Strong data management, analytical and problem-solving skills, including experience handling large-scale longitudinal research data and maintaining high standards of data quality and accuracy. Excellent organisational, communication and stakeholder management skills, with the ability to coordinate multiple priorities and work effectively across multidisciplinary research teams and collaborating organisations. Experience working in a research-intensive environment, including supporting research administration, literature reviews, data reporting, and the preparation of research outputs and project documentation. A proactive and collaborative approach, with strong attention to detail, sound judgement, and the ability to work independently while contributing positively to team objectives. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 05 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Exam-Administration-Officer_0157037-1": {
      "title": "Exam Administration Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMultiple casual opportunities supporting the scanning and processing of examination papers for the Faculty of Engineering, University of Sydney Paid, in-person onboarding and training provided on 12 November HEO 4 $59.13 per hour plus super   About the opportunity  The Faculty of Engineering are seeking three casual Exams Administration Officers to support the processing and scanning of examination papers during the Semester 2 examination period. Working closely with the Faculty's Education Support Team, you will play an important role in ensuring examination papers are accurately processed, scanned, reconciled, and securely uploaded for marking.   These positions are based at the Faculty of Engineering (J12) on our Darlington Campus and will support the upcoming examination period, which will run from 16th – 28th November 2026 (including Saturdays 21 and 28 November) and again from 14 to 18 December. Please include your availability across these dates in your application.   Your key responsibilities will be to:  Work closely with the Exams Office and Education Support Team to process examination papers  receive and reconcile examination papers against attendance records  prepare examination papers for scanning, including removing staples and sorting documents  scan examination papers, ensuring completeness and accuracy upload scanned examination papers to secure University systems  maintain accurate records and follow examination handling procedures.   About you  Demonstrated experience undertaking administrative or operational support activities strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. ability to maintain continuous mobility for extended periods, including frequently transporting examination papers to and from printers   experience handling high-volume processing tasks with a focus on accuracy, quality, and attention to detail.  confidence using Microsoft Office and online document management systems  ability to use initiative and exercise sound judgement in relation to role responsibilities.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Leah Edwards, Recruitment Operations by email to leah.edwards@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 15 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer--Teaching-and-Research--School-of-Public-Health_0156473-2": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Teaching and Research)-School of Public Health",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.60 FTE) continuing role at Camperdown Senior Lecturer (Teaching and Research) in Clinical Epidemiology -School of Public Health Base Salary $ 162,347 (pro rata for 0.60 FTE) + 17% superannuation plus clinical loading (pro rata 0.60 FTE) About the opportunity Founded in 1930, the Sydney School of Public Health is Australia’s first and leading school in public health, dedicated to addressing the world's most pressing health challenges. Each day, our researchers and graduates are tackling global health issues and striving to improve equity in communities both locally and around the world. We are seeking an outstanding Senior Lecturer in Clinical Epidemiology who will contribute to excellence in teaching, research, leadership and service within the School of Public Health and the Master of Clinical Epidemiology program. Your key responsibilities will be to: Build and maintain a reputation for academic excellence through independent and innovative contributions to research and education, expanding knowledge and practice in the discipline, with a particular focus on advancing clinical epidemiology, evidence-based healthcare, and research methodologies relevant to clinical and population health establish collaborations and partnerships to enhance the research profile and reputation of the university, including with health services, clinical networks, research institutes, and partners within the Westmead Health and Research Precinct and Centre for Kidney Research coordinate and engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the university’s expectations and standards for education, including supervising higher degree research students to successful completions, and contributing to the delivery of the Master of Clinical Epidemiology program and related postgraduate coursework offerings coordinate, develop and continually improve curricula, programs and resources to enhance teaching and learning outcomes, including incorporating contemporary clinical epidemiology methods, digital learning resources, innovative pedagogical approaches, and authentic assessment practices provide leadership that has a significant impact on teaching, innovation, curriculum development, research training and supervision within the school or faculty, including leading curriculum renewal and applied learning initiatives within clinical epidemiology education understand and contribute to furthering the university and faculty/school strategic agenda. establish partnerships, collaborations and engage in translational research for impactful outcomes in research, development, commercialisation, and policy improvement, including investigator-led and collaborative multicentre clinical research studies and clinical trials lead and contribute to competitive research funding applications and develop an internationally recognised program of research in clinical epidemiology and chronic disease provide academic leadership in the design, coordination and delivery of capstone, project-based or applied learning experiences that prepare students for real-world clinical research and healthcare settings strengthen interdisciplinary and translational research initiatives through collaboration across academic, clinical, and healthcare sectors, supporting improved health outcomes and health equity. About you To be successful in this role, you will have: a PhD in Clinical Epidemiology, Public Health, or a closely related discipline experience teaching within a Master of Clinical Epidemiology or equivalent postgraduate program and coordinating high-quality postgraduate units current AHPRA registration and substantial experience in Australian clinical practice and healthcare settings demonstrated expertise in clinical epidemiology methodologies, including diagnostic and screening test evaluation, and clinical research design significant experience leading curriculum development, course renewal, and innovative teaching approaches across online, blended and face-to-face learning environments a strong research track record in clinical epidemiology, including experience leading or contributing to clinical research studies, clinical trials, and high-quality peer-reviewed publications demonstrated success, or strong potential for success, in securing competitive research funding and developing an impactful research program experience supervising Higher Degree by Research students to successful completion and mentoring early-career researchers expertise in clinical research relevant to chronic disease and the ability to contribute to interdisciplinary and translational research initiatives highly developed interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build productive relationships across multidisciplinary teams, health services and academic environments demonstrated contributions to academic leadership, governance, professional service and administration within higher education, healthcare organisations, or professional bodies the ability to develop and strengthen partnerships with clinical networks, particularly within the Westmead Health and Research Precinct and the Centre for Kidney Research. Desirable experience conducting multisite clinical research and participating in national or international research collaborations established links with health service providers and research organisations, particularly within the Westmead Health and Research Precinct experience contributing to professional learning, micro-credential, or modular educational initiatives. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey,  Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 25 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Scientific-Software---Computing-Support-Technician_0154779-2": {
      "title": "Scientific Software & Computing Support Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position. Located at the Physics Building (A28) on the Camperdown Campus Opportunity to utilise your experience in the Technical Support Services Team   Base Salary HEO6 $107,312 - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Scientific Software & Computing Support Technician supports the Scientific Computing Team in supporting a diverse range of custom computing infrastructure and delivers bespoke technology support for Faculty of Science equipment. This can include associated computing hardware/firmware, operating systems, applications, software, storage, and related ‘hands-on’ support. The position is a field-service customer interface for the Scientific Computing Team working under general direction as a routine contact point for receiving, handling, and resolving a broad range of support incidents and requests. This role will deliver timely and effective technical services within these areas to achieve enhanced education/research support outcomes.      The Scientific Software & Computing Support Technician will assist in implementing new processes, procedures, systems, and ways of working within the work area to improve customer service delivery, efficiency and resource utilisation as directed. The technician will provide training to facility users in the safe operation of equipment, software, and computing infrastructure. The role supports the senior technical officer in ensuring that the relevant Work, Health and Safety standards and other compliance requirements of the work area are met and are aligned to the University’s WHS policy and relevant legislation.      The Scientific Software and Computing Support Technician will provide guidance to junior staff in the work area to deliver work outputs.  The successful candidate will be required to work on-site four days per week, with one day per week available for remote work (work from home). Your key responsibilities will be to: support the agreed delivery of technical services in the designated area  support the implementation of service improvements and service excellence within the responsibility area  contribute to a positive, professional, customer-focused team culture  support the effective utilisation of resources within the designated area of responsibility, ensuring the education and research needs of the work area are consistently and effectively met champion best practices, workplace health and safety, compliance, and risk management   seek and act on opportunities to cross-skill, including drawing on the knowledge and expertise of others  provide project management or project support as required, collaborating with other TSS, PSU or central project managers/teams as required.   About you tertiary qualifications or certifications in computing field and/or relevant experience. A background in Science at a University level is desirable   excellent working knowledge of computing, peripheral hardware, operating systems (Windows/MacOS), troubleshooting issues and configuration in desktop/workstation and VM (AWS, Azure) environments extensive hands-on experience installing, configuring, and troubleshooting software, hardware and dependencies in a research environment including imaging and deployment technologies    experience managing network connectivity, cybersecurity, and storage, with proficiency in command line use and scripting languages for system administration and troubleshooting  demonstrated ability to establish and maintain working relationships with customers   ability to work both independently and as part of a team ability to apply and contribute to the development, maintenance, and review of standard technical documentation and operating procedures.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow---Sleep-and-Neuropsychology_0156459-3": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Sleep and Neuropsychology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term for two years at Camperdown Opportunity to contribute to internationally recognised research in sleep, ageing and dementia risk Base Salary Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Sleep and Neuropsychology to join a multidisciplinary research team focused on sleep, ageing and cognitive health. This position will work under guidance of Professor Sharon Naismith play a key role in coordinating and delivering research projects investigating sleep and ageing in older adults at risk of dementia. Working within a collaborative research environment, you will contribute to high-quality research outcomes, support project delivery, engage with participants and stakeholders, and help advance evidence-based approaches to understanding the relationship between sleep, neuropsychology and healthy ageing. Your key responsibilities will be to: coordinate research projects focused on sleep, ageing and dementia risk in older adults design, implement and manage research activities in accordance with approved protocols collect, analyse and interpret quantitative and/or clinical research data contribute to the preparation of research publications, reports and presentations support participant recruitment, assessment and retention activities collaborate with academic, clinical and industry partners as required assist with research ethics, governance and regulatory compliance requirements contribute to grant applications and other research funding initiatives. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Research Fellow who has: PhD in Psychology, Neuropsychology, Neuroscience, Sleep Science or a closely related discipline demonstrated experience conducting research in sleep, ageing, cognitive neuroscience, neuropsychology or dementia-related fields experience coordinating complex research projects and managing multiple priorities strong quantitative data analysis skills and experience with relevant statistical software evidence of research excellence through peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations experience working with older adult populations and/or clinical research participants is desirable demonstrated ability to work collaboratively within multidisciplinary research teams excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage a range of stakeholders. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate---Radio-Frequency-Fingerprinting-using-Machine-Learning_0156651-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Radio Frequency Fingerprinting using Machine Learning",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 12 months Opportunity to advance research in AI Hardware Acceleration Base Salary $93,424 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the University of Sydney is seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to an exciting project focused on the challenge of creating robust radio frequency fingerprinting (RFF) systems using machine learning. The successful candidate will have significant experience in signal processing or machine learning, and an outstanding track record in conducting research. You will join a team of researchers that are pushing the boundaries on this topic. You will have access to our state-of-the-art equipment and facilities and connect with leading researchers worldwide. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver outcomes in a research project and ensure that milestones are met work with colleagues and postgraduates within the project prepare materials for publications in journals and conferences collaborate with industry partners and leaders in the field of radio frequency fingerprinting. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Researcher in Radio Frequency Fingerprinting using Machine Learning who has: strong experience in signal processing, machine learning or a related field a PhD (or near completion) in signal processing, machine learning or a related field experience in the development of machine learning models using Python and pytorch expertise in two or more of the following technical areas: implementation of signal processing systems, data collection for machine learning, wireless physical layer, radio electronics, explainable AI strong problem solving and analytical skills. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Saturday 26 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Sydney/School-General-Manager_0155541": {
      "title": "School General Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing leadership opportunity as School General Manager   Play a key leadership role in shaping the future of one of Australia's leading music institutions through collaboration and partnership   Senior Professional Staff position offering a competitive remuneration package, including 17% superannuation     About the opportunity      One of Australia's oldest and most prestigious music institutions, the Sydney Conservatorium of Music has been part of the University of Sydney since 1990, providing world class music education, performance opportunities, and research. For more than a century, it has been at the heart of Australia's musical and cultural life, playing a pivotal role in shaping the nation's musical landscape.     This is an exciting time to join the Sydney Conservatorium of Music, which continues to gain national and international recognition for excellence in music education, performance, and research. Adding to this momentum, the Conservatorium has announced the first expansion in its 110 year history, extending its world class programs and industry partnerships to Western Sydney through a new Parramatta campus.     The Sydney Conservatorium of Music is seeking an exceptional School General Manager to join its leadership team. This influential role offers the opportunity to help shape the Conservatorium's academic and operational direction, partnering with senior leaders to deliver on its strategic vision and goals.   Your key responsibilities will include:   As a key member of the leadership team and a vital link between the Conservatorium and the University's central services, this role is instrumental in driving operational excellence, leading strategic initiatives, and ensuring the infrastructure, resources, and processes are in place to support exceptional teaching, learning, research, music and performing arts programs and performances , and external engagement outcomes.   Strategic leadership and planning: Partner with the Head of School and Dean to translate the School's strategic vision into operational priorities, driving planning, decision-making, and cross-University collaboration to achieve key objectives   Operational leadership and management: Lead the implementation of University -wide operational initiatives and service improvements within the School , ensuring alignment with strategic priorities and successful adoption across all areas. Oversee the operational planning and delivery of music and performing arts programs, performances, events, and related activities to support the School's academic and artistic objectives .   People leadership and management: Provide strategic leadership of the School's professional services function, driving workforce planning, recruitment, talent development, succession planning, and the implementation of University people policies to foster a high-performing and future-focused team   Financial management and oversight: Partner with the Head of School and Dean and the Finance team to develop, manage, and monitor the School's budget, ensuring alignment with financial strategy and performance objectives .     About you     You are a collaborative and influential operational leader with the ability to partner effectively with academic leadership, navigate complex environments, and deliver organisational priorities through collaboration, innovation, and sound decision-making.     relevant tertiary qualifications, or an equivalent combination of experience and training   significant senior leadership experience in operations, business management, or professional services leadership, ideally gained within higher education, the creative arts, research-intensive institutions, or similarly complex environments   experience in the administration and operational management of music and/or performing arts programs, productions, performances, and related activities within a higher education, arts, or cultural institution would be highly regarded   proven experience leading and developing diverse teams, fostering an inclusive, high-performing, and service-oriented culture   strong financial management , planning, budgeting, and resource allocation capability   demonstrated success in delivering strategic initiatives, operational improvement programs, and organisational change   exceptional communication, negotiation, stakeholder engagement, and influencing skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships across a broad range of stakeholders   strong analytical and problem-solving capability, using data and insights to inform decision-making and drive organisational outcomes.   Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 . EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Justyn Griffin, Recruitment Operations by email to justyn .griffin@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 11 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Head-of-School--School-of-Social-and-Political-Sciences_0154103-1": {
      "title": "Head of School, School of Social and Political Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOpportunity for a distinguished academic to lead the School of Social and Political Sciences Be a part of the leadership team of the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences Fixed-term role for a period of 5 years with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at the University of Sydney is seeking to appoint a distinguished academic as Head of School, School of Social and Political Sciences who will join the Faculty’s leadership team. The Head of School is responsible for the School’s academic teaching, learning and research performance outcomes that support the Faculty’s strategic goals, maintaining accreditation standards, the student experience, meeting financial targets and workplace culture. The position has formal financial and people delegations and is responsible for effective decision making to ensure financial sustainability. The successful applicant will contribute to the recruitment, appointment, development and retention of talented staff who are able to attract research funding and develop students across the School’s programs. The successful applicant will also help to promote the School’s, the Faculty’s and the University’s reputation for excellence and its national and international academic standing. The successful candidate will also play a role in ensuring that the Faculty meets its goals as set out in the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences Strategic Plan . The of School of Social and Political Sciences is home to one of the most successful academic groups in the world for research and teaching in the social sciences. Through a suite of undergraduate and postgraduate programs - including Criminology, Social Justice, Sociology, Political Economy, Anthropology, Public Policy, Politics, International Relations and International Security, as well as the new Bachelor of Politics, Philosophy, and Economics - the School delivers leading edge education in a tailored social science learning environment. The School is also home to a quality PhD cohort that works together with academic staff on some of the most urgent questions and problems facing the world today. These include the future of democracy, multispecies justice, asset-based inequalities and endemic health crises. Through a rich variety of multidisciplinary networks and research centres, our academics maintain significant connections with the international social science community and with our diverse partners. The School also has a number of distinguished alumni and is proud of its connections into the local community. Our academic staff and our students are exceptionally well placed to explore the social, cultural, economic and political foundations of the contemporary world. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The successful candidate will hold a PhD qualification and will have a recognised international academic and professional standing within a relevant academic field. You will have extensive experience in people leadership and management within the higher education/knowledge worker sector, experience in working with industry, and a track record in managing financial sustainability, infrastructure and the operational needs and practices of an academic unit. Your ability to make strategic decisions, together with a strong business acumen, will support your endeavours in academic leadership, key stakeholder management and industry collaborations. Paramount to success in the role is a leadership style that builds trust across a diverse academic community, together with the judgement to navigate complex stakeholder environments, competing priorities and the consultative processes of a large institution. The successful applicant must have a wide network of established relationships with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders, that can be leveraged to enhance the standing of the School, the Faculty and the University. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa Sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The term of the appointment is 5 years with the possibility of renewal, subject to negotiation. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline in the School and will retain that position at the conclusion of their headship. An attractive remuneration package will be offered. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Brochure: Candidate Information Brochure Click to view the Position Description  for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sajad Bahram (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email at sajad.bahram@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 11 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Digital-Experience-and-Campus-Support-Liaison--Targeted-_0147067-2": {
      "title": "Digital Experience and Campus Support Liaison (Targeted)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time / Continuing position   Location: Multi-site (metropolitan & regional campuses) across NSW   Base salary from $117,133 – $127,610 + 17% Superannuation     This is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.     About the opportunity     Join the Digital Engagement function within Information and Communications Technology (ICT) as a hands-on technical liaison connecting metropolitan and regional sites with ICT. You’ll provide Tier 2 support, build trusted relationships with staff and turn local technology needs into lasting service improvements.     Your key responsibilities will be to:     Partner with Digital Engagement, Service Management, Client Technology, Field Services and AV Services to support local ICT needs.   Provide hands-on Tier 2 technical support across desktops, laptops, mobile devices and integrated systems.   Run scheduled site visits and regional rotations, identifying local technology issues, risks and service gaps.   Build trusted relationships with academic, professional and teaching staff as a familiar point of contact for ICT advice.   Investigate recurring incidents and root causes, translating local insights into actionable service improvements.   Travel to regional locations as required, with some trips potentially involving several days or up to two weeks away from the main campus.     Why work with us   We are committed to creating a culturally safe, supportive and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Key benefits include:   Generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements. Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding. Participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support and professional development opportunities. YURA network (promoting sharing of experiences, and professional support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff members) Tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund     About you   Strong hands-on technical skills, with experience diagnosing and analysing complex IT issues across desktops, laptops, mobile devices and integrated systems.   Tier 2 technical experience providing escalated support and advice for IT infrastructure, live events and hybrid teaching environments.   Experience working independently in remote, regional or under-resourced locations while maintaining consistent technical support.   Experience identifying trends, recurring user issues and root causes and implementing preventative service improvements. Experience in a multi-site, distributed, multi-campus, higher education or public sector environment.   Relevant qualifications or equivalent experience in ICT, with industry-recognised ICT certifications desirable.     How to apply     If   you're   looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.    Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any   additional   supporting documentation) can be   submitted   via the   Apply   button at the top of the page .       For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your   Workday   account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard .    Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Accessibility and inclusion    The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you   require   adjustments or   additional   support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.    This may include adjustments to the interview format,   additional   time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you   participate   comfortably.    In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact   Camila Garcia ,   on   email to   camila.garcia@sydney.edu.au   email address.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are   trust, accountability and excellence   and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a   University   community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to   diversity and inclusion ,   evidenced   by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,   women ,   people living with a disability ,   people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as   LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.       We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.   Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      Pre-employment checks    Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all   role   required   pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.    Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia    You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment .      ©   The University of Sydney    The University reserves the right not to   proceed   with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 24 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Development-Associate_0156262-1": {
      "title": "Development Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nWe now have an exciting new opportunity for a Development Associate, Faculty Arts and Social Sciences . In this role you will play a role in securing philanthropic investment that will support projects and programs in these portfolios and across the University . Drawing upon your relationship building skills, th ere will be a focus on identifying new prospective donors for the strategic priorities of the deans, building on recent successful gifts, and growing the pipeline of donors .   You will engage high net-worth donor prospects, align their interest and passions to the strategic priorities of the University, and successfully solicit multiple gifts with a value of $100,000 or more.   While we welcome applications from all candidates, t his is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority.    About FASS    The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences is consistently ranked among the leading faculties of its kind, nationally and internationally and has a proud history and tradition of intellectual rigour, committed to offering a vibrant research and teaching environment.   The mission of the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences is to conduct research and teaching across the breadth of the humanities and social sciences that makes a difference in the lives of its students, staff and the broader community, both in Australia and globally.   About you     The University values trust, accountability and excellence. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Development Associate, Faculty Arts and Social Sciences who:       is a natural relationship builder – you enjoy interacting with people one-on-one and fostering relationships with individuals at different levels and from diverse cultures;   has excellent communication skills, both listening and written, enabling you to develop compelling proposals or cases for support that align with a prospective donor’s interests;   possesses highly developed emotional intelligence , you are self-aware with exceptional interpersonal skills; an active listener with a proactive work ethic;   is a high-achiever , naturally driven by success both professionally and personally;   is confident in handling data with confidentiality and integrity;   is passionate about biomedical, healthcare, or medical research sectors, and you can demonstrate this in a cover letter, along with explaining why you would be a strong candidate for this role.   About us     The Advancement Portfolio team at the University of Sydney is one of the most successful fundraising program in Australian higher education. Having completed the nation’s first $1BN fundraising campaign and onto the next , the team is not only the finest performing, its success facilitates the largest real-world impact that philanthropic giving makes possible.    Since its inception 1 75 years ago, the University of Sydney has led to improve the world around us. We believe in education for all and that effective leadership makes lives better. These same values are reflected in our approach to philanthropy and underpin our long-term strategy for excellence and growth. We’re Australia's first university and have an outstanding global reputation for academic and research excellence.        How to apply     Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position and for recruitment-related enquiries, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact   Sarah Scott,   Recruitment Partner (Advancement Portfolio), at   sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au           Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. For a continuing position, you must be an Australian or New Zealand citizen or an Australian Permanent Resident.    Pre-employment checks     Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   The University of Sydney is committed to diversity and social inclusion. Applications from people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds; equity target groups including women, people with disabilities, people who identify as LGBT IQ ; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged.        © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 04 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Head-of-Research-Insights---Analytics_0156512": {
      "title": "Head of Research Insights & Analytics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing position within the Advanced Analytics, Planning and Enterprise Data team Lead the University's research analytics capability, driving research excellence, impact and performance through data-driven insights Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University is seeking a Head of Research Insights and Analytics to lead and shape the institution's research analytics capability. This will entail defining how research evidence, data and intelligence are used to inform strategy, improve performance, and advance research excellence and impact across all disciplines. Reporting to the Chief Data and Analytics Officer and leading a specialist team, you will partner with the Research Portfolio, faculties and senior academic leaders to deliver strategic research insights that support evidence-based decision-making, research investment and institutional priorities. You will drive the continual evolution of research analytics, reporting, benchmarking, visualisation and self-service capabilities, while ensuring research data remains trusted, secure, compliant and fit for purpose. This role is ideal for an experienced analytics leader who combines strategic thinking, strong stakeholder engagement and deep technical expertise to translate complex data into meaningful actions and measurable outcomes. You will play a critical role in strengthening the University's research performance, supporting major external assessments and enhancing its national and global standing.             Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the development and execution of the University's research analytics strategy, aligning research intelligence with institutional priorities and performance objectives partner with academic and professional leaders to deliver actionable insights that inform research strategy, resource allocation and investment decisions oversee analytics and reporting for major external assessments and submissions, including rankings and benchmarking exercises drive innovation in research reporting, visualisation, predictive analytics and self-service capabilities to improve access to trusted insights establish robust data governance, quality and compliance frameworks that ensure the integrity, security and reliability of research information lead, develop and mentor a high-performing analytics team while fostering a culture of collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement monitor emerging trends in research policy, funding, assessment and analytics to identify opportunities and risks that may impact research performance and institutional outcomes. About you significant experience in a senior leadership role within a research analytics, data analytics, institutional planning or comparable function in a large and complex organisation demonstrated success leading and developing high-performing analytics teams and building organisational capability in data-driven decision-making proven ability to engage and influence senior executives, academic leaders and diverse stakeholder groups through the delivery of strategic insights and advice extensive experience in analytics, reporting, performance measurement, benchmarking and data governance frameworks strong understanding of contemporary analytics practices, including predictive analytics, visualisation and self-service reporting environments exceptional communication, presentation and stakeholder management skills, with the ability to translate complex analytical concepts into clear business outcomes. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nayomi Siriwardane, Recruitment Operations by email to nayomi.siriwardane@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 28 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Animal-Technician_0155163-2": {
      "title": "Animal Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing role located across the different Laboratory Animal Services facility locations of Camperdown and St Leonards and other facilities as required Opportunity to provide research excellence through high level animal welfare Base Salary of $93,761 + 17% superannuation About the team Laboratory Animal Services is a Research Infrastructure professional service unit. We are a team of Animal Technicians, Facility Officers and Veterinarians who provide day-to-day care for research animals, maintain animal research facilities, prioritise animal welfare and help ensure regulatory compliance on the Camperdown and St Leonards campuses as well as other facilities in Sydney. We work in partnership with researchers and faculties to provide services to support and enable high-quality biomedical research in areas including: Chronic Diseases Molecular Biomedicine Infection Immunity and Inflammation Cancer Neuroscience Mental health Obesity Diabetes Cardiovascular disease  About the opportunity As the Animal Technician , you will provide animal husbandry services and will monitor and implement relevant policies and procedures to ensure appropriate animal welfare standards and regulatory compliance is met in day-to-day operations. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide sound technical research support and assistance to researchers, students and other technical staff working in the facility maintain accurate and up to date animal and facility records communicate concerns relating to animal welfare promptly and assist in resolving any issues as directed ensure the animal facilities are compliant with Workplace Health and Safety and regulatory requirements participate in an overtime roster providing routine inspections on weekends and delivery of essential animal husbandry tasks on public holidays. About you qualifications in a relevant field or the equivalent in training and experience animal handling and animal care skills of a high standard, especially in relation to common laboratory species and experience with SPF principles a commitment to animal welfare and to the use of animals in research conducted in accordance with the NSW Animal Research Act and a practical understanding of the outcomes sought by relevant regulatory code attention to detail with strong record-keeping skills a willingness to work as part of a team and flexibility to work in any of Laboratory Animal Services’ facilities (including availability to work additional hours or to participate in a weekend roster as required) a valid Australian Driver’s License is desirable general overall fitness with the ability to be able to lift and push up to 15kg. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University including attending a pre-commencement medical assessment. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey, Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Executive-Assistant---Project-Officer-to-the-Head-of-School---Dean-Sydney-Nursing-School_0156383-2": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant & Project Officer to the Head of School & Dean-Sydney Nursing School",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time fixed term from 30 November 2026 until 15 January 2027 An exciting opportunity for an Executive Assistant to provide confidential, high level and secretariat support to the Head of School at Sydney Nursing School Base Salary $ 107,312 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity The University of Sydney Susan Wakil School of Nursing and Midwifery (Sydney Nursing School) is the school of choice to nurture global citizens in health and wellbeing. We are currently ranked #2 in Australia and #19 globally in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. At the School we seamlessly integrate teaching, research, and scholarship with practice and policy development, while fostering engagement with all health professions. Our experienced staff, many of whom work in frontline healthcare services, make a real difference to the healthcare landscape in Australia and internationally. The Executive Assistant and Project Officer will make independent decisions in relation to day-to-day activities and will make other decisions within the scope of this position. This role will utilise excellent judgement and problem-solving skills to resolve issues and will use critical judgement to raise important issues for consultation with the Head of School and Dean and the School Manager. Your key responsibilities will be to: coordinate and manage the daily functions of the Head of School and Dean (HOSD), including their responsibilities as Head as well as professorial, professional and industry responsibilities liaise with government and other external bodies in the scheduling of appointments, making complex travel arrangements and maximising the productive use of available time issuing invoices for reimbursement of travel and acquitting expenses in Concur assist the School Manager in the provision of high-level administrative support perform executive secretarial duties including prioritisation, dissemination, and preparation of non-routine correspondence organize the weekly schedule of the Head and provide and maintain appropriate documentation for all engagements including the preparation of summary briefs when required organize HOSD meetings and provide secretariat support management of the approval process across the school for documents requiring HOSD approval. delivering on school administration projects as determined by the School strategic plan and School Manager. About you extensive experience in providing executive administrative support to senior executive staff demonstrated computing skills in email (Outlook), the internet and Microsoft Office (including PowerPoint, SharePoint, Teams and Power BI) experience in University systems including Concur, Workday and Unibuy familiarity with research software including Scopus ID, ORCID iD demonstrated ability to adapt to a changing and evolving workplace to meet the school and University’s requirements ability to establish and maintain excellent working relationships with a wide range of people, both internal and external to the University and with professional bodies ability to independently compose correspondence without supervision and draft minutes and agendas ability to work both independently and as part of a team, taking initiative and exercising judgement in resolving matters that may arise as part of normal daily work excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills with sound negotiating and conflict resolution skills and a demonstrated level of empathy and discretion in dealing with complex matters. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer--Clinical-Education-_0154817-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Clinical Education)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.6 FTE) continuing opportunity for a Senior Lecturer (Clinical Education) Opportunity to lead innovation in clinical education by teaching into the Master of Surgery Program Base Salary $162,347 (Pro Rata) + 17% superannuation + Full clinical loading pro-rata for eligible candidates About the opportunity The Senior Lecturer (Clinical Education) is an education-focused academic role that makes a significant contribution to teaching and learning through the application of extensive clinical experience and expertise. You will play a leading role in developing and implementing contemporary clinical pedagogy, driving innovation in curriculum design, and strengthening partnerships with the surgical profession and stakeholders. Working collaboratively with academic colleagues and industry partners, you will contribute to high-quality surgical education and help advance the strategic goals of the School, Faculty and University. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the development and implementation of innovative education initiatives and curriculum review activities deliver high-quality teaching within the Mastery of Surgery program, with a focus on surgical anatomy and surgical sciences coordinate and contribute to teaching and learning activities that meet University standards for education excellence supervise research students and support successful student outcomes coordinate award programs and lead team-teaching activities with academic colleagues develop innovative teaching resources and contemporary clinical learning practices mentor and support clinical educators while leading clinical teaching projects and initiatives build and maintain productive relationships with clinical, industry and professional partners contribute to strategic planning, academic leadership and continuous improvement activities across the School and Faculty. About you Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons (FRACS)  experience with anatomy teaching and postgraduate surgical education significant experience applying contemporary clinical teaching practices demonstrated expertise in education, curriculum design, and pedagogy experience coordinating and delivering high-quality clinical teaching and learning activities experience supervising and mentoring students and colleagues ability to establish and maintain effective relationships with professional and industry stakeholders excellent communication, presentation, organisational and influencing skills. Work Rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence, you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to commencing employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to obtain and maintain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion, evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People, women, people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, and those who identify as LGBTIQ+. We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please log into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustments or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger, Recruitment Consultant, via email at Danielle.Selinger@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Sydney/Clinical-Research-Orthoptist_0155470-2": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Orthoptist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time 0.6 FTE as a Clinical Research Orthoptist, until 14 October 2027 Located at Sydney Eye Hospital Base salary from $93,345 (pro-rata), plus 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Clinical Research Orthoptist reports to the Clinical Research Orthoptist Team Leader to ensure the efficient and effective delivery of delegated clinical trial group activities, (pharmaceutical company sponsored and investigator-initiated), following set protocols and ensuring accuracy and compliance with critical regulations. This position is designated as child-related employment. To be able to work in this role, you will need a current Working With Children Check as well as an updated and compliant Vaccination Record card, in line with NSW Health employment requirements (Category A). Your key responsibilities will be to: implement clinical trials under the supervision of the Clinical Research Orthoptist Team Leader develop and maintain relationships with clinical trial stakeholders, participating in research meetings and facilitating communication amongst various entities  engage in quality and compliance activities to ensure clinical trial documents, ethics applications, participant data capture and verification align with GCP and relevant SOPs lead by example in prioritising work, health and safety within daily operations, coordinate with peers for safety drills and training, and proactively identify areas for safety improvement within the work scope. About you bachelor’s or master's degree in Orthoptics recent experience in the clinical trials setting including HREC requirements, ICH-GCP and study coordination. Sound organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities, multi task and to meet deadlines. Ability to consistently show initiative and work proactively Ability to communicate effectively and engage in teamwork and collaboration. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  We encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people to apply. If you’d like to learn more about the role or our approach before applying, we’re happy to have an informal yarn or chat. We want the application process to feel accessible and supportive — please reach out if you’d like to talk it through. As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements (Category A) This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations on by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Armytage-Postdoctoral-Fellowship_0156186": {
      "title": "Armytage Postdoctoral Fellowship",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term 18 months C ontribute to an innovative and interdisciplinary research program at Sydney Law School Academic Level A Base Salary $ 93,424 - $ 125,896 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity Sydney Law School invites applications for the inaugural Armytage Postdoctoral Fellowship to contribute to an innovative and interdisciplinary research program examining harmful child and adolescent sexual behaviours in contemporary digital contexts. This exciting research initiative addresses a significant and under-examined challenge at the intersection of children's rights, sexual violence, coercive control, technology-facilitated harm and justice responses. The project seeks to generate new knowledge about the pathways through which children and young people engage in harmful sexual behaviours, the role of digital technologies and online environments in shaping these behaviours, and opportunities for prevention, early intervention, rehabilitation and systems reform. Situated at the intersection of law, criminology, psychology, child protection, gender-based violence, digital harms and social policy, the research responds to pressing social and policy challenges arising from rapidly changing online and offline environments. The successful Fellow will contribute to the development of an emerging national and international research agenda focused on children's safety, wellbeing and rights. Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct high-quality interdisciplinary research on harmful child and adolescent sexual behaviours in digital and offline contexts design, lead and contribute to research activities, including data collection, analysis and dissemination of findings develop an independent research program aligned with the broader project and build an emerging research profile collaborate with academic, government and community stakeholders to support research impact and knowledge exchange contribute to publications, presentations, reports and other research outputs that inform law, policy and practice engage with national and international research networks and support the development of the project’s research agenda teach into the school’s LLB/JD or PGCW programs for a six-month period at 0.2 FTE. About you PhD in law or criminology, or a related discipline with demonstrated experience in conducting empirical research (please disregard the educational qualification requirement outlined in the Position Description and refer to this requirement when assessing eligibility) demonstrated experience conducting original research and producing high-quality scholarly outputs strong interest in interdisciplinary research related to children's rights, digital harms, sexual violence, justice or child protection ability to develop and lead an independent research program within a collaborative research environment excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to disseminate research to diverse audiences demonstrated ability to build productive relationships with academic, government and community stakeholders experience teaching or tutoring in a relevant area of LLB/JD or PGCW curricula, or the capacity to do so effectively. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rae Hao, Recruitment Operations by email to rae.hao@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lawyer--Intellectual-Property--Research-and-Commercial-Law_0141110-1": {
      "title": "Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing opportunity Located at the Michael Spence Building (F23) on the Camperdown Campus Base Salary $162,528 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney’s Office of General Counsel (OGC) is looking to recruit an experienced and motivated Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law to join the IP, Research and Commercial Law Team. The Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law will provide clear, practical and timely legal advice to primarily support the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) and the Research Portfolio progress the University’s research and commercialisation activities. Working closely with stakeholders across the Research Portfolio—including Pre-Award and Pipeline, Post-Award (Grants and Contracts), Clinical Trial Contracts, Commercialisation and Investments—the successful candidate will manage and support senior lawyers with a diverse range of matters involving the review, drafting and negotiation of research and commercial agreements, support for strategic projects, commercialisation initiatives, and other legal issues as they arise. All work will be undertaken with a commercial and strategic mindset, aligned with OGC’s Purpose and Strategy. The Lawyer, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law will work under the direction and support of a Senior Lawyer in the Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law Team, and will collaborate closely with senior colleagues across the Team and the wider OGC, including the Director, Intellectual Property, Research and Commercial Law and the General Counsel. Your key responsibilities will be to: help Research Portfolio clients navigate complexity to give decision makers confidence in pursuing the University’s objectives and strategy provide clear, timely, strategic legal advice to to support the activities of the Research Portfolio, with the support of Senior Lawyers and the Director, IP, Research and Commercial Law draft, review and negotiate research and commercialisation agreements (e.g. funding agreements, research collaboration agreements, clinical trial research agreements, data and material transfer agreements, professional services agreements, IP agreements (e.g. assignments, options, licenses) and transactions, etc.) to promote the University’s strategic interests and objectives manage external legal services, as required  contribute to other legal work, projects and initiatives as part of the broader remit of the IP, Research and Commercial Law team and OGC, as required  advise on legal developments which may impact University policies and practices, particularly relating to research and commercialisation. About you qualifications in law and admission to practice in Australia, and hold a current practicing certificate a minimum of 6+ years post admission legal experience is desired  experience and practice of intellectual property law, commercialisation and commercial law, with research experience (with clinical trials experience or knowledge looked favourably upon), with plaint English drafting, negotiation and matter management skills an understanding of the legal and regulatory framework relating to research and the major Australian public funding schemes available for research highly developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to operate with tact and professionalism across complex internal and external environments  team player – experienced collaborating in a team environment  an agile and flexible approach that is able to pivot and contribute to overall program success growth mindset – not afraid to challenge the status quo. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nayomi Siriwardane, Recruitment Operations by email nayomi.siriwardane@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Special-Events-Project-Manager_0156329-1": {
      "title": "Special Events Project Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAbout the opportunity   It is an exciting time to join the Alumni and Supporter Experience team that provides an innovative engagement program for a community of over 450,000 people.   The Special Events Project Manager provides strategic project management, leadership and delivery expertise across a portfolio of engagement, cultivation, stewardship and community-focused events and special projects within the Advancement Portfolio. While the Community Festival is the flagship project and primary focus of the role, the incumbent also leads, contributes to, and provides specialist advice on a range of event initiatives throughout the year.   About you   Proven experience in coordinating the delivery of a broad range of events, including high-profile and large-scale events   You are a confident stakeholder manager with a passion for building and maintaining relationships  Advanced verbal and written skills with a keen attention to detail  Previous project management and administration experience  A natural ability to think critically and resolve complex issues  High-level organisational and time management skills, including ability to prioritise workload and work well under pressure  Ability to adapt to a fast paced and changing event environment.  About us   Since 1850, the University of Sydney has been shaping a better future through education, discovery and leadership. Today, our community extends far beyond campus, encompassing hundreds of thousands of alumni, donors and supporters around the world. The Alumni and Supporter Experience team brings this community together through events, programs and initiatives that celebrate achievement, strengthen connections and inspire support for the University's mission. Why The University of Sydney   When you join the University of Sydney, you become an integral part of a community in which you are fully supported to achieve your career ambitions. You’ll be in a workplace where development is encouraged, and intellectual pursuits foster a sense of purpose and confidence including:  •    35-hour work week  •    flexible working model  •    up to 36 weeks paid parental leave  •    access to health and wellbeing services  •    tax-efficient salary packaging options  •    ongoing training and development  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. For a continuing position, you must be an Australian or New Zealand citizen or an Australian Permanent Resident.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.  EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.   Closing Date: 11:59 Sunday 20th September How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Sarah Scott, Recruitment Operations by email to sarah.scott@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney     The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.  Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Concord/Director--ANZAC-Research-Institute_0155786-3": {
      "title": "Director, ANZAC Research Institute",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity for an outstanding senior academic who is a leading authority within their discipline Fixed term position for 5 years with opportunity for further appointment with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The ANZAC Research Institute is a biomedical research institute where discovery meets care. Guided by our history, we partner with clinicians to innovate and deliver improved health outcomes for Defence members, veterans and our broader community. The Institute was established in 1995 and is an entity of Sydney Local Health District (SLHD) operating as a research partnership with the University of Sydney. It is located on the campus of Concord Repatriation General Hospital (CRGH), a primary referral hospital within SLHD and a teaching hospital of the University of Sydney. We are seeking to appoint an outstanding academic at the level of Associate Professor or Professor (Level D or E) as the Director of the ANZAC Research Institute. The appointee will have overall responsibility for leading the implementation of the Institutes strategic plan, provide scientific guidance, and liaise with SLHD and Precinct Academic Directors on operational and financial management of the Institute. In accordance with the research collaborative agreement, the Director will have accountability to the Chief Executive of SLHD and the Dean of Faculty of Medicine and Health University of Sydney (or their appointed delegates from time to time) as designated employers of the Director. In the case of the Faculty of Medicine and Health, the Director will report to the Head of School Medical Sciences, one of seven schools in FMH. The Director will also provide regular reports to the ANZAC Advisory Council covering the overall research performance of the Institute. The position is to be based in the School of Medical Sciences at University of Sydney and the Director will be a member of the school’s leadership team. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The successful candidate will: hold research doctoral degree (PhD, MD or equivalent) with an outstanding record of achievement in a field of biomedical research relevant to the Institute (aging or related health specialties). have proven ability to deliver research an international standard in one or more fields relevant to the research of the Institute. have an outstanding research track record as evidenced by publications, instances of research translation and/or commercialisation supervision of successful HDR students. demonstrate evidence of peer recognition through involvement and leading committees, boards and editorial appointments and track record of developing national & international collaboration. have proven ability to secure significant and sustained competitive research funding. have proven ability to deliver on strategic directions of the Institute and provide scientific leadership within a complex biomedical research environment. Capacity to promote the research and translation functions of the Institute with the SLHD and University. understand research governance policies and legislation and rules of ethical research conduct. promote a strong research culture, strengthening existing collaborations and fostering new partnerships. be managing engagement with industry partners. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and Relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a 5-year fixed term full-time position, subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees.  There is opportunity for further appointments subject to performance. The successful candidate will be offered an attractive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Other As this position is located on a NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure for further details. Candidate Information Brochure For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position from: Professor Mary Collins, Head of School, School of Medical Sciences, mary.collins@sydney.edu.au  Faculty of Medicine and Health. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact: Dr Mushtaq Sobhan (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au   Applications Close Friday 09 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Project-and-Administration-Officer---NSW-Brain-Tissue-Resource-Centre_0155004-3": {
      "title": "Project and Administration Officer - NSW Brain Tissue Resource Centre",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, fixed term until June 2027 Exciting opportunity to be a Project Officer with Professor Greg Sutherland Base Salary starting from $93,345 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for medical science education in the world. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas. The NSW Brain Tissue Resource Centre (BTRC) is a specialised research facility located within the Charles Perkins Centre , within the School of Medical Sciences . The BTRC collects and stores clinically and pathologically characterised human post-mortem brain tissue to support research into neurological and psychiatric disorders, including alcohol addiction. The Project and Administration Officer will support the BTRC by coordinating the associated brain donor program (Using our Brains). through organising project activities, managing stakeholder relationships, supporting brain donation processes and assisting with research data management. The role will contribute to strategic communication initiatives that build community awareness, strengthen partnerships and improve outcomes for affected Australians. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide project management support for the Using our Brains donor program, including coordinating timelines, reporting, meetings, events and promotional activities support governance requirements including ethics, privacy and data storage processes in line with health research standards coordinating enrolment and characterisation of brain donors manage research project data, including database maintenance, data entry, reporting and ensuring data integrity, confidentiality and secure record management contribute to the analysis and dissemination of quantitative and qualitative research findings provide professional administrative support to the project team, donor community and public, including responding to enquiries and preparing documentation build and maintain productive relationships with university stakeholders, external partners, health practitioners and community groups develop and implement communication strategies to promote the donor program and facilitate information sessions and presentations coordinate project documentation, records management and administrative processes to support effective program delivery undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required. About you a health, neuropsychiatric or clinical practice-related employment background experience working in the higher education sector or a complex, multi-faceted organisation understanding of ethical, privacy and confidentiality requirements relating to health research and brain donation excellent organisational and administrative skills with the ability to prioritise competing tasks and deadlines ability to work independently and collaboratively, using initiative and sound judgement to resolve day-to-day matters excellent interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills with the ability to build relationships with diverse stakeholders experience managing stakeholder relationships across large or complex projects and developing productive partnerships NSW Driver’s License or ability to drive in NSW. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Indigenous-Health---Identified_0152027-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Indigenous Health - Identified",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-Time Continuing opportunity for a Lecturer in Indigenous Health (Identified) Contribute to the design, development, and implementation of First Nations curriculum across the Faculty of Medicine and Health Base Salary $132,533 + 17% superannuation This position has been designated for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW). Should your application be progressed, you will be required to provide your confirmation of identity documents. Your recruiter will be in touch to advise you on how to confirm Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander status. About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander academic to join Sydney Medical School as a Lecturer in Indigenous Health (Identified) . This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to the education of future health professionals while advancing Indigenous health education, scholarship, and community engagement. Sydney Medical School has a long-standing reputation for excellence in education, research, and healthcare leadership. The School teaches more than 2,000 undergraduate students and 1,200 graduate medical students and is committed to preparing clinically outstanding, research-capable, and globally aware graduates. Indigenous health is a core theme embedded throughout the medical curriculum, reflecting the University's commitment to improving health outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. As a Lecturer in Indigenous Health , you will apply your scholarly expertise and contemporary educational practices to design, deliver, and evaluate high-quality learning experiences. You will make a significant contribution to teaching and learning, support student success, and help strengthen Indigenous health education across the curriculum. Working collaboratively within a multidisciplinary team, you will contribute to the design, development, and implementation of First Nations curriculum across the Faculty of Medicine and Health. The role supports ongoing curriculum transformation, promotes culturally safe educational practices, and plays an important part in advancing health equity and improved outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities. This position offers the opportunity to influence the next generation of healthcare professionals while contributing to meaningful educational change in one of Australia's leading medical schools. Your key responsibilities will be to: contribute to the design, development, and delivery of First Nations curriculum across medicine and health programs undertake teaching and curriculum-related academic work supporting the Indigenisation of health curriculum collaborate with academic, professional, First Nations and community stakeholders to implement faculty-wide curriculum initiatives support the development of culturally safe, strengths-based, and responsive learning experiences and assessment practices contribute to capability-building activities, including workshops, resources and staff development in cultural safety and decolonising pedagogies assist with coordination, evaluation and continuous improvement of First Nations curriculum activities and scholarly outputs. About you demonstrated experience in teaching and/or curriculum development within health or higher education knowledge of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health, cultural safety and strengths-based approaches to healthcare experience working collaboratively with diverse stakeholders, including First Nations communities and organisations emerging or demonstrated contribution to research, scholarship or evaluation in education or Indigenous health ability to contribute to culturally responsive curriculum design, assessment, and program-level initiatives commitment to advancing Indigenous strategy, health equity, and culturally safe education practices.   Why work with us: At the University of Sydney, we are committed to fostering a culturally safe, supportive, and inclusive workplace where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff can thrive, contribute, and lead. Recognised as an employer of choice for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, we understand that the strength of our university is enriched by the skills, expertise, perspectives, and cultural knowledges of First Nations people. We are committed to creating opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff to shape teaching, research, leadership, and community engagement across the institution. As one of Australia's leading universities, we value strong and meaningful relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities. These connections are fundamental to our ongoing commitment to excellence, cultural responsiveness, and positive social impact. Our aspiration to be an employer of choice is reflected in the University's strategic commitment to embedding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledge, cultures, histories, and perspectives into everything we do. Through this commitment, we continue to create an environment where First Nations staff are supported to achieve their professional goals while contributing to lasting educational and community outcomes. Joining the University of Sydney means becoming part of a community dedicated to advancing Indigenous leadership, strengthening cultural capability, and making a meaningful contribution to improving outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Key benefits include: generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support, and professional development opportunities YURA network (promoting sharing of experiences, and professional support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff members) tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. This is a targeted opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact  Danielle Selinger, by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Project-Manager---InsideOut-s-Upskilling-Project_0155988": {
      "title": "Project Manager - InsideOut's Upskilling Project",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.4 FTE), fixed-term opportunity until 30 November 2027, based on Camperdown Campus Support the delivery of a nationally significant mental health initiative and help strengthen eating disorder identification and early intervention across Australia Base Salary $131,108 (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity Join the InsideOut Institute , Australia's national institute for research, clinical excellence, and policy innovation in eating disorders, and contribute to initiatives that strengthen the capability of mental health services to identify and respond to eating disorders across the country. We are seeking an experienced Project Manager to lead the delivery of the Upskilling the Mental Health Workforce in Evidence-Based Eating Disorder Interventions project. This is an opportunity to play a key role in embedding eating disorder care pathways across commonwealth funded mental health services nationally, and strengthening the capability of Australia’s mental health workforce through the delivery of evidence-based training, digital interventions and clinical capacity-building initiatives. Working with leading partners including headspace National, headspace centres, Medicare Mental Health services and Primary Health Networks, you will provide end-to-end project leadership across a nationally focused initiative. You will oversee project planning, delivery, stakeholder engagement, governance, reporting and financial management, ensuring the project achieves its milestones and delivers meaningful outcomes for people experiencing eating disorders. You will work across research, digital and clinical streams and collaborate with a broad network of mental health professionals and organisations. Your expertise will help translate evidence into practical workforce development initiatives that strengthen access to high-quality eating disorder care across Australia. Your key responsibilities will be to: Lead the end-to-end planning, delivery and evaluation of the Upskilling Project, ensuring activities are delivered within scope, budget and agreed timeframes. Develop and oversee project plans, governance structures, operating models, milestones, performance indicators and risk mitigation strategies to support effective project delivery. Build and maintain strong partnerships across the mental health sector. Oversee the implementation of eating disorder digital innovations across services nationally. Oversee the national rollout of eLearnings, clinician webinars and digital interventions, working closely with research and digital teams to support evidence-based workforce development and sustainable program uptake. Monitor project performance, financials, quality and outcomes, leading reporting to funding bodies and key stakeholders and supporting research and evaluation activities. Ensure project activities comply with University of Sydney, InsideOut Institute and Sydney Local Health District governance requirements, while identifying opportunities to strengthen delivery, impact and sustainability. About you Postgraduate qualifications in project management. Project management qualifications with an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience. Extensive experience leading complex projects from planning through to delivery and evaluation, with strong skills in project planning, organisation, risk management and financial oversight. Demonstrated ability to engage, influence and negotiate effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders, building collaborative relationships across complex environments. Strong critical thinking and decision-making skills, with the ability to identify issues, manage competing priorities and develop practical solutions to support successful project outcomes. Excellent communication and teamwork skills, with experience working collaboratively across multidisciplinary teams and managing relationships with a range of stakeholders. Experience working within Australia’s mental health service system, with knowledge of key sector partners, and an understanding of initiatives that improve access to care and outcomes for people experiencing eating disorders. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Executive-Support-Assistant---Targeted_0145336-2": {
      "title": "Executive Support Assistant - Targeted",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nProvide professional executive support to the ICT Leadership Team, coordinating critical activities and day-to-day administration   Support Executive ICT leaders through diary management, meetings, correspondence,   travel   and stakeholder coordination   Salary:   $93,345 - $104,515 +   17% Super  ​ This is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.   About the opportunity     The Executive Support Assistant provides professional executive and administrative support to the ICT Leadership Team. Working closely with senior ICT leaders and the Executive Support Officer, the role helps ensure day-to-day requirements are managed efficiently,   effectively   and confidentially.   The role requires someone who is proactive and comfortable operating with autonomy ;   anticipating   what is needed, progressing work without waiting for detailed instruction and ensuring important actions do not fall through the gaps.     Your key responsibilities will be to:     W orking closely with the ICT Leadership Team and Executive Support Officer to provide a cohesive and efficient service. P roviding executive and administrative support to the ICT Leadership Team. M anaging incoming enquiries and correspondence,   prioritising   and escalating matters where appropriate.   ​ C oordinating meetings, agendas, papers, minute-taking and logistical arrangements. P reparing correspondence, reports,   documentation   and briefing notes.   M anaging travel arrangements and itineraries in line with   University   policies and procedures.   M aintaining administrative, document and records management systems. C ontributing to operational improvement activities and effective teamwork   Why work with us   We are committed to creating a culturally safe,   supportive   and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Key benefits include:   17% Superannuation G enerous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements   A ccess to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding   p articipation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer   support   and professional development opportunities   YURA network (promoting sharing of experiences, and professional support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff members)   T ax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and   additional   contributions to your superannuation fund     About you:   Substantial experience providing proactive executive support to senior leaders, preferably in a large and complex   organisation .   Extensive experience managing complex and   frequently   changing executive diaries, including negotiating competing priorities and resolving scheduling conflicts.   Demonstrated ability to   anticipate   requirements, work with minimal   direction   and take ownership of matters   through to   completion.   Sound judgement and discretion, including the ability to independently resolve day-to-day matters and   identify   when escalation is   required .   Confidence in engaging with senior internal and external stakeholders, following up   actions   and appropriately negotiating or redirecting requests.   Strong written communication and attention to detail, with experience preparing correspondence, meeting papers,   reports   and briefing material.   A   practical,   adaptable   and solutions-focused approach, with the ability to remain composed and effective as   priorities   change.   S trong capability in Microsoft Office, email,   calendar   and travel-booking systems.     How to apply     If   you're   looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.    Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any   additional   supporting documentation) can be   submitted   via the   Apply   button at the top of the page .       For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your   Workday   account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard .    Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Accessibility and inclusion    The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you   require   adjustments or   additional   support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.    This may include adjustments to the interview format,   additional   time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you   participate   comfortably.    In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact   Camila Garcia ,   on   email to   camila.garcia@sydney.edu.au   email address.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are   trust, accountability and excellence   and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a   University   community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to   diversity and inclusion ,   evidenced   by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,   women ,   people living with a disability ,   people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as   LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.       We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.   Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      Pre-employment checks    Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all   role   required   pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.    Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia    You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment .      ©   The University of Sydney    The University reserves the right not to   proceed   with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 23 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Clinical-Psychologist-Brain-and-Mind-Centre_0155510-2": {
      "title": "Clinical Psychologist-Brain and Mind Centre",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.40FTE) fixed term until 4 August 2027 (maternity leave cover) at Camperdown We are seeking an experienced Clinical Psychologist to support mental health interventions for young people at headspace Camperdown Base Salary $ 117,133 (pro rata for 0.40 FTE)+ 17% superannuation  About the opportunity headspace Camperdown is a comprehensive youth health service that provides physical and mental health support, information and services to young people aged 12-25 and their families in a youth friendly environment and is led by the Brain and Mind Centre . The team at headspace includes clinical psychologists, psychologists, general practitioners, social workers, occupational therapists, mental health nurses, researchers and administration staff. headspace Camperdown is seeking an experienced and passionate Clinical Psychologist to deliver high-quality assessment, engagement, and care coordination services to young people aged 12 to 25 years. Working as part of a multidisciplinary team, you will support young people with diverse mental health, physical health, vocational, and substance use needs through an early intervention approach. Experience working with eating disorders is highly desirable Your key responsibilities will be to: Provide psychological assessment and evidence-based psychological therapy for young people (12-25) with emerging or established mental health disorders under an early-intervention (clinical staging) framework of service delivery in collaboration with the headspace team, provide follow up assessments for young people with emerging mental health disorders using a standardised assessment format work with a high level of independence & autonomy in coordination & administration of a busy youth mental health service ability to liaise closely with onsite independent practitioners, local referral agencies and other services carry out all tasks with professionalism, tact, sensitivity, confidentiality and discretion and work calmly under pressure; Problem solve decisively and responsibly in dealing with difficult situations including clinical issues maintain accurate and timely case notes and records inputting data required by headspace National and headspace Camperdown adhere to headspace Camperdown Clinical Governance Framework, policies and procedures using IT systems including Microsoft Office, Email, Practice Management Software provided onsite e.g. Blue Chip and Medical Director, and data collection tools as determined by the Brain and Mind Research Institute and headspace National Office. Ensure data entry compliance. participate in relevant Clinical Meetings to participate in headspace Camperdown community awareness activities promoting the health and wellbeing of young people and liaise with relevant community groups, government and non government service providers. participate in quality improvement projects and research maintain a working knowledge of current legislation and government policy in relation to mental health and alcohol and other drug disorders, particularly as it relates to young people in NSW participate in training and professional development as required by headspace, and any other tasks appropriate to classification as requested by the supervisor. About you Postgraduate qualifications - Clinical Psychology AHPRA registration as a psychologist with an endorsed area of practise in clinical psychology experience in psychological assessment and intervention services for young people aged 12-25 years who have mental health, alcohol and other drug problems competence regarding clinical information systems, software packages and willingness to be trained and implement MDS, the headspace data collection and registration tool computer literate, with familiarity with the Microsoft Windows environment and Microsoft Office applications and capacity to learn how to use a new clinical software package for keeping of electronic client records demonstrated ability to consult, liaise and negotiate with young people, family/carers, workers and other agencies well developed interpersonal and communication skills (written and verbal), problem solving, advocacy and negotiation skills. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 15 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Casual-Academics--Administration-Officer_0149507-1": {
      "title": "Casual Academics' Administration Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMultiple roles available in Pay Compliance Analysis Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base salary from $107,312 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The new Workplace Operations team works closely with academic and professional colleagues to ensure the University meets its operational and compliance obligations for casual academic staff. The team drives continuous improvement, embeds consistent   best ‑ practice   processes and frameworks, and oversees operational compliance across all faculties and business areas—ensuring cohesive, rigorous standards and   accurate ,   timely   pay outcomes in line with   University   policies, enterprise   agreements   and legislative requirements.   The   Casual Academics’ Administration Officer (CAAO)   plays a key role in delivering   high ‑ quality   operational support that ensures casual academic staff are   allocated   work correctly, paid accurately, and that all associated processes meet audit and compliance standards. Working closely with stakeholders,   you’ll   manage work allocations, timesheet reconciliations,   enquiries   and discrepancy case investigations, while   providing clear, practical guidance on the compliance controls framework and processes. A strong analytical mindset is essential, as you will routinely interpret data,   identify   anomalies, assess   risks   and contribute insights that strengthen operational compliance   and controls framework efficacy . This   role suits an organised,   detail ‑ focused   administrator who enjoys problem solving, managing   deadlines   and contributing to a strong operational culture in a   fast ‑ paced   higher ‑ education   environment.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   manage work allocations, and timesheet reconciliation and investigations   with   high levels   of accuracy, analytical rigour ,   consistent process   application   and strong compliance judgement   resolve enquiries and support compliant pay outcomes , using data to   identify   anomalies ,   trends   and risks, and escalat e   non-compliance   promptly   maintain complete,   audit ‑ ready   documentation and records   in line with enterprise agreements,   policy   and operational controls   support   pre ‑ semester ,   pay ‑ cycle   and   data ‑ quality   activities , ensuring   timely ,   accurate   and   analytically sound   compliant   delivery   deliver   high ‑ quality   administrative support that enables reporting, insight generation and continuous improvement , including identifying patterns ,   a nd pr oactive ly   ra i sing   compliance   risks and process gaps   contribute to process reviews, testing and operational enhancements   that strengthen compliance, data   integrity   and team capability.   About you   demonstrated administrative experience in a busy,   deadline ‑ driven   environment, with the ability to   self ‑ manage   workload and flag risks early   strong attention to detail and accuracy, with confidence managing   high ‑ volume , routine   processes   and data updates   compliance-focused,   able to interpret requirements of   framework and apply controls consistently   analytically minded, able to interpret data,   identify   anomalies   and   root ca uses , investigate   discrepancies   and provide clear,   evidence ‑ based   insights   clear, professional communicator able to manage enquiries and collaborate effectively within a complex network of stakeholders   experience working with systems,   data   and records management, with a commitment to   maintaining   complete and compliant documentation   organised, adaptable and comfortable navigating competing priorities during peak periods   proactive,   solutions ‑ focused   and committed to delivering quality service while modelling compliant behaviours and contributing positively to team culture   a nd cont inuous   impro vement .   Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah , Recruitment Operations by email to sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 15 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Executive-Officer--Indigenous-Strategy-and-Services_0156665-1": {
      "title": "Executive Officer, Indigenous Strategy and Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until 1st December 2027 Play a key role supporting Indigenous Strategy and Services leadership and governance Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation About the opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for an experienced Executive Officer to join the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Indigenous Strategy and Services (DVC ISS). Working closely with the Executive Director, Indigenous Strategy and Services, you will provide high-level strategic, governance and operational support across the portfolio, helping drive initiatives that advance Indigenous outcomes, reconciliation and organisational priorities across the University. Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with the Executive Director, Deputy Vice-Chancellor (ISS) and senior stakeholders across the University provide strategic advice, planning support and policy guidance across the Indigenous Strategy and Services portfolio coordinate governance, compliance, risk management and reporting activities for key portfolio initiatives prepare executive briefings, discussion papers, reports and committee papers for senior leadership forums monitor and report on the delivery of strategic priorities, projects and operational plans build and maintain effective relationships with faculties, portfolios and key internal stakeholders to support collaboration and outcomes. About you demonstrated experience providing executive-level support and strategic advice to senior leaders within a large and complex organisation extensive experience in governance, policy development, project management and organisational planning demonstrated experience supporting budget planning, forecasting and financial reporting, including monitoring financial performance and providing advice on financial risks and priorities within a complex organisation demonstrated understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, communities and contemporary Indigenous matters knowledge of governance and management structures within the higher education, vocational education and/or government sectors relevant postgraduate qualifications, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience demonstrated experience building stakeholder partnerships and influencing outcomes across diverse organisational environments. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Education-Support-Officer_0155934-2": {
      "title": "Education Support Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term for two years Opportunity to deliver a broad range of project related administrative and operational function at the University of Business School HEO 6 Base Salary $107,312 - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Education Support Officer works within the Business School’s Learning Innovation Office (LIO) to deliver a broad range of project related administrative and operational functions. These include communications from LIO to the wider Business School and to project stakeholders, oversight of contracts with external service suppliers, management and payment of invoices, and support for the design and development of online learning materials in the University’s learning management system. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide administrative and operational support for learning innovation and digital learning projects, ensuring activities are delivered efficiently and in line with project objectives coordinate project communications and maintain effective engagement with stakeholders across the Business School and wider University support the planning, implementation and delivery of strategic projects, including tracking activities, milestones and outcomes manage project-related procurement, invoices and financial records, ensuring accurate reporting and compliance with University processes monitor and coordinate contracts with external suppliers and service providers to support successful project delivery assist in the design, development and quality assurance of online learning materials within the University’s learning management system maintain project documentation, records and reporting systems, while providing high-quality administrative support to the Learning Innovation Office team. About you (selection criteria) bachelor's degree, or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience ability to provide administrative and project support within a higher education institution or similarly complex organisation strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, work under pressure and meet deadlines excellent written and interpersonal communication skills ability to build productive relationships and engage effectively with a diverse range of stakeholders ability to manage procurement, invoices, budgets or financial records, with strong attention to detail and accuracy ability to support online learning activities via a learning management system, including content administration, quality assurance and the delivery of digital learning resources ability to work independently and collaboratively, using initiative and sound judgement to achieve outcomes efficiently and effectively. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a C over Letter directly addressing each point listed under the “About you (selection criteria)”, a CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rae Hao, Recruitment Operations by email to rae.hao@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 22 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Project-Manager---Workstream-Lead_0155972-1": {
      "title": "Project Manager / Workstream Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\n• Full time, fixed term position until Feb 2028 • Lead a high-impact data governance workstream that strengthens privacy, compliance and organisational resilience • Base Salary $152,045 - $160,430 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for an experienced Workstream Lead to join the Privacy and Resilience Program, leading a key workstream focused on improving the University's data governance and privacy practices. Working across a complex, decentralised environment, you will drive the delivery of initiatives that identify, assess and reduce privacy risks by coordinating data discovery, governance and remediation activities across the University. Your key responsibilities will be to: Plan and lead workstream responsible for identifying, assessing and remediating data stored across Microsoft 365 staff environments including Outlook and OneDrive lead the delivery of a workstream within the Privacy and Resilience Program, ensuring outcomes are achieved on time and to a high standard coordinate data discovery and remediation activities to identify and reduce large volumes of personal sensitive information held across University systems develop plan, methodology and process requirements to scale delivery and activities across enterprise engage with stakeholders to identify data owners, support retention requirements and embed effective information governance practices coordinate project activities across technical and business teams, managing priorities, risks, dependencies and reporting work closely with technical and communications specialists to prepare business, drive adoption and deliver data management and governance initiatives contribute to the continuous improvement of privacy, operational risk and information governance frameworks across the University About you 8–10+ years Project Management experience Microsoft 365 implementations or remediation programs desirable demonstrated experience leading complex projects or workstreams, ideally within data governance, information management, privacy or risk management strong understanding of data management principles, including information governance, data ownership, retention and privacy risk proven ability to coordinate technical project delivery while effectively engaging business stakeholders experience working within complex, decentralised organisations with multiple stakeholder groups tertiary sector experience is highly desirable experience with Microsoft Visio and data scanning technologies will be highly regarded Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Laura Pierce, Recruitment Operations by email to laura.pierce@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Eveleigh/Software-Engineer_0155459-2": {
      "title": "Software Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, fixed term contract for 2 years Participate in a wide ranging and multi-disciplinary research program Base Salary starting from $ $117,133 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Image X Institute is a centre for innovation in radiation therapy and medical imaging technologies. Our research portfolio spans development of new treatment ideas, clinical trials, preclinical imaging, device development, MRI-Physics, radiation therapy physics, and computational physics. We engage with industry, hospitals, international collaborators and universities to forge relationships that help take our projects from lab bench to patient bedside. Here, you will have the opportunity to participate in a wide ranging and multi-disciplinary research program. We are seeking a Software Engineer ( HEO 7)  to join the Image X Institute to advance technologies that improve thoracic and abdominal imaging technologies. This position is part of a program funded by an NHMRC Ideas Grant focused on liver cancer treatment. You will lead and contribute to the development and clinical translation of image guidance technologies. Your key responsibilities will be to: Lead the development and maintenance of software for cutting-edge technologies in real-time image-guidance of the thorax and abdomen Curate databases and code central to the aims of the grant Author high-impact publications and present at international conferences Support students and junior researchers Develop software solutions, ensuring scalability and performance. Coordinate the architectural design and system planning for software projects Oversee the testing process and implement automated testing frameworks Coordinate deployment pipelines and ensure efficient software deployment Create and maintain detailed technical documentation for software Foster open communication and collaboration within the team, addressing stakeholder queries and concerns in a timely manner, while building trust within immediate work networks Oversee report compilation to meet established standards, proactively identify compliance improvements, and collaborate with the team to rectify discrepancies Monitor risk within the team, promote workplace health and safety, ensure alignment with university policies, and proactively address potential challenges. About you A Masters in Software Engineering, IT, Computer Science, or a related discipline Experience with medical imaging or devices Experience integrating novel technologies with clinical systems Experience contributing to or working with teams Recognition through awards and scholarships Experience With testing frameworks and front end and back end development of web-based applications Delivering solutions in an Agile/Scrum environment. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Associate-Lecturer-Geosciences--PhD-Teaching-Fellow-_0156235-1": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer Geosciences (PhD Teaching Fellow)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart time, 2-year fixed term position, located on the Camperdown Campus Excellent opportunity to develop into the next generation of Australian University academics Base Salary Level A $93,424 p.a. (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity MUST BE ENROLLED IN A DEGREE AT THE UNIVERSITY OF SYDNEY The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is offering an exciting opportunity for a PhD Teaching Fellow to become part of its academic community. This role allows candidates to engage in teaching and learning activities while continuing to pursue their individual research.  We are looking for current PhD student to join us in a part-time (0.2 FTE) Associate Lectureship roles in the Geosciences. Eligible candidates must be enrolled in an appropriate PhD program in the School of Geosciences within the University of Sydney and anticipate maintaining their enrolment for the duration of their appointment. As a PhD Teaching Fellow you will: engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education-focused staff contribute directly to the delivery of in-person practical classes, tutorials, workshops and other learning activities in Geosciences, with a particular focus on our large first-year units contribute to the development, deployment and evaluation of learning resources for students and training resources for casual academic staff engage constructively with student feedback and other evaluations of teaching practice suggest, implement and evaluate improvements and innovations in teaching and learning develop and understand best practices in teaching and learning attend and contribute to School/Faculty meetings and contribute to events such as Open and Info Days contribute to education-related projects aligned to the strategy of the Faculty. Training will be provided by Faculty staff and teaching mentors. Additionally, you will have access to various University training courses and modules on topics such as team teaching, creating positive environments, and peer-to-peer learning. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for PhD Teaching Fellows who: has an Honours or Masters degree in Geoscience or relevant related areas are enrolled in a PhD degree at the University of Sydney has a passion for teaching, with previous tutoring experience highly preferred have a strong skill set in Geographic Information Systems (GIS). possess good time management and organisational skills has excellent communication skills, both written and verbal are motivated and able to work independently have experience using Canvas (or equivalent learning management systems) and other online learning Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cherie Goodwin or Rebecca Astar, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 05 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Integration-Developer---ETL_0154540-3": {
      "title": "Integration Developer - ETL",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin the University's ICT Data and Integration Services team as an experienced Integration Developer - ETL in this 12-month fixed-term opportunity Design, develop and optimise enterprise ETL/ELT and data integration solutions that deliver secure, scalable and high-quality data pipelines Salary of $131,108 to 147,858 with additional career development opportunities and 17% superannuation Why join the University of Sydney? Join one of Australia's leading universities at a pivotal time, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, teaching excellence, and research at global scale. ICT is central to the University's mission, delivering the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact. With strong investment and ambitious transformation underway, you'll work in a large and complex environment where your contribution will have visible, meaningful impact across the University community. The opportunity The Integration Developer - ETL works with the Senior Manager - Integration Services, Data and Integration Team members, solution architects, and various business stakeholders to develop and implement integration solutions to solve business challenges. The role designs, develops, automates, and supports complex applications to extract, transform, and load data. It will translate data access, transformation, and movement requirements into functional requirements and mapping designs. The role is responsible for the design of the logical data model and implements the physical database structure and constructs and ensures data quality. It will create and enhance data integrations enabling seamless delivery of data and is responsible for collecting, parsing, managing, and analysing large sets of data ensuring it adheres to the Governance Process, Integration Standards, and best practice. The role is required to understand and review technical requirements, design ETL process flow and walkthroughs and perform data profiling of required source systems. What you'll do Design, develop and deliver high-quality, scalable ETL and data integration solutions using Informatica, supporting the ingestion, transformation and management of large, complex datasets. Build and deploy robust ETL processes across on-premises and cloud environments, ensuring reliable, secure and efficient data integration solutions. Collaborate with Integration Designers, developers and business stakeholders to implement data strategies, develop data flows and deliver solutions that meet business and technical requirements. Drive quality and best practice by applying integration standards, governance, DevOps principles and participating in code reviews to deliver secure, maintainable and reusable solutions. Design, build and test complex interfaces and data pipelines spanning multiple enterprise applications, ensuring data integrity, performance and compliance. Support and continuously improve enterprise integration services by resolving production issues, implementing enhancements, maintaining technical documentation and contributing to a culture of continuous improvement. What we're looking for Tertiary qualifications in Information Technology or in a relevant field or related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant training and demonstrated experience. Demonstrated analytical and problem-solving skills , with the ability to troubleshoot complex technical issues and deliver practical, high-quality solutions. Strong experience across the data integration development lifecycle , including requirements analysis, solution design, ETL development, testing, deployment and ongoing support. Sound knowledge of enterprise data integration and system interfaces , with experience designing and implementing reliable, scalable integration solutions. Advanced SQL expertise , including developing and optimising complex queries, stored procedures and database performance. Experience designing and developing relational database solutions , with a strong understanding of database design principles, data modelling and best practices. What's in it for you? 17% superannuation Generous parental leave entitlements Salary packaging options Flexible and hybrid working arrangements Career development and learning opportunities Health and wellbeing programs, including Fitness Passport Access to a vibrant, globally recognised university community How to apply If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you. Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Accessibility and inclusion The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know. This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kimberley Smith, kimberley.smith@sydney.edu.au . EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
