    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer--Clinical-Education-_0156441-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Clinical Education)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.2) FTE continuing opportunity for 2 Clinical Education Lecturers - Education Focused Roles Opportunity to contribute to high-quality postgraduate medical education in critical care medicine Base Salary $132,533 - $152,406 + 17% superannuation + Full clinical loading pro-rata for eligible candidates About the opportunity The Faculty of Medicine and Health is seeking 2 Lecturers (Clinical Education) to contribute to innovative teaching and learning within postgraduate medical education in critical care medicine. Play an important role in delivering high-quality educational experiences, supporting student learning, and contributing to the ongoing enhancement of curriculum, assessment, and teaching practices. The role will focus on online postgraduate coursework programs and collaboration with academic colleagues to deliver outstanding educational outcomes. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake teaching activities, applying relevant clinical expertise to support student learning contribute to the design, delivery and continuous improvement of teaching, learning, and assessment materials engage in innovative and contemporary educational practices that enhance the student experience provide mentoring and academic guidance to students support and collaborate with academic colleagues to promote effective teaching practice contribute to faculty meetings, administrative activities and strategic initiatives as required maintain productive relationships with relevant professional, industry and academic stakeholders participate in on-campus activities, with an expectation of approximately 50% face-to-face attendance undertake professional development in higher education teaching, including completion of a Graduate Certificate in Higher Education or an approved fellowship pathway where required by the University. About you: grad cert or AFHEA/FHEA is preferred/highly desirable postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline, or equivalent professional expertise demonstrated experience in clinical teaching and learning environments understanding of contemporary teaching practice and curriculum design principles experience mentoring, supervising or supporting learners and colleagues excellent communication, presentation and stakeholder engagement skills strong organisational skills and ability to work effectively within a collaborative academic environment current professional expertise and experience relevant to the discipline or clinical context. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Senior-Housing-Officer_0156284-2": {
      "title": "Senior Housing Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing role. Located at our Camden Campus Provide support to our student accommodation operations Base Salary HEO6, $107,312 - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Senior Housing Officer, Student Accommodation to oversee the day-to-day operations of the Camden Student Accommodation portfolio, including Nepean Hall, Nepean Lodge and Nepean Cottages, while also providing operational oversight of the Kingswood student accommodation complex and other University-managed or affiliated accommodation sites as required. Reporting to the General Manager, Accommodation Operations and working closely with the Manager, Business Systems and Relationships, this role supports occupancy, revenue performance, maintenance coordination and stakeholder engagement across the accommodation portfolio, ensuring assets are well maintained and operations run effectively. The Senior Housing Officer, Student Accommodation will work collaboratively with the Student Life team (Wellbeing and Cocurricular) to ensure a high level of customer service, support and to enhance the overall student experience. The successful candidate may be required to travel to the main Camperdown Campus as requested by the Accommodation Operations General Manager. Your key responsibilities will be to: oversee the day-to-day operations of Camden Student Accommodation and support operational oversight of Kingswood and other allocated accommodation sites monitor occupancy, reservations, revenue performance and operational outcomes to maximise utilisation and service delivery across the accommodation portfolio coordinate applications, bookings, peak accommodation periods and emergency accommodation requirements while ensuring a high standard of customer service and student support manage maintenance, refurbishment and asset lifecycle activities, including contractor coordination, to ensure accommodation assets are maintained to University standards support financial administration, including revenue reconciliation, journal processing, forecasting, budget preparation, budget monitoring and financial reporting supervise and support site-based staff and work collaboratively with accommodation providers, contractors and internal stakeholders to achieve operational objectives support compliance with relevant legislation, University policies, residential agreements and operational procedures while promoting resident safety and well-being contribute to continuous improvement initiatives through the review of accommodation operations, systems, maintenance programs and stakeholder feedback processes. About you a bachelor’s degree in a relevant field or equivalent work experience a First Aid Certificate strong financial and operational management skills, including budgeting, reporting and occupancy forecasting experience in site leadership and coordination, ideally within student accommodation, facilities or hospitality environments broad understanding of real estate principles and compliance with the Residential Tenancy Act extensive project management experience ranging in size and complexity including understanding of the quoting and tendering process, timeline management, contractor management and compliance requirements excellent stakeholder management skills and the ability to foster productive working relationships. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Challis-Chair-of-Jurisprudence_0155353-3": {
      "title": "Challis Chair of Jurisprudence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing opportunity  Prestigious Chair dedicated to Jurisprudence, Legal theory and the Philosophy of Law Offering a competitive Academic remuneration package including 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking an outstanding scholar to join the Sydney Law School as the Challis Chair of Jurisprudence . This flagship named Chair is one of the oldest at the University and was the fourth of its kind in the English-speaking world. As such, it offers the occupant a unique opportunity to shape the future of jurisprudence, legal theory, and the philosophy of law at one of the world's leading research-intensive universities. As Challis Chair, you will provide intellectual leadership that strengthens the School's global standing, advances an ambitious research agenda, and fosters excellence across teaching, research and engagement. Working collaboratively with colleagues, you will play a strategic role in the governance of the School while cultivating an inclusive and vibrant research culture that supports scholars at every career stage. The successful candidate will be recognised as a leading voice in jurisprudence, bringing the vision, momentum and international profile to further elevate the University's reputation for excellence in legal scholarship. Your key responsibilities in the role will be to: provide internationally recognised academic leadership in jurisprudence, legal theory, law and philosophy, or the philosophy of law develop and deliver an ambitious, world-leading research program that enhances the University's global reputation publish high-quality research in leading international journals and academic presses attract competitive research funding and foster collaborative research initiatives across Australia and internationally provide intellectual stewardship for jurisprudence and legal theory within the school, supporting researchers across early, mid and senior career stages contribute strategically to the leadership, governance and future direction of the Julius Stone Institute of Institute and the School develop and maintain strong international scholarly networks that enhance the visibility and influence of the University's jurisprudence program deliver high-quality teaching and research supervision across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, inspiring the next generation of legal scholars contribute thoughtfully to contemporary debates about the role of jurisprudence in legal education, legal scholarship and professional practice engage with the broader legal, academic and public communities through scholarly leadership and public intellectual engagement mentor and support academic colleagues, fostering a collaborative, high-performing and inclusive academic environment. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) You will be an internationally recognised academic leader or an emergent leader on an exceptionally strong trajectory towards global leadership whose scholarship demonstrates originality, intellectual rigour and influence within jurisprudence, legal theory, law and philosophy, or the philosophy of law. You will combine academic excellence with the ambition to shape the field further and inform the future direction of one of Australia's leading law schools. To be successful, you will demonstrate: a PhD in Law, Philosophy or a closely related discipline current appointment at Professor level (Level E) or Associate Professor level (Level D), or having attained an equivalent record of academic achievement and scholarly distinction justifying appointment at Professor level an established or an exceptionally promising emergent international reputation for research excellence in jurisprudence, legal theory, law and philosophy, or philosophy of law an outstanding publication record in internationally recognised journals and with globally respected academic publishers demonstrated success in securing competitive external research funding and leading high-impact research initiatives and/or collaborations the ability to provide strategic academic leadership and contribute to the governance and long-term development of a research-intensive law school experience mentoring and developing researchers across all career stages, fostering research excellence and academic capability a collaborative and intellectually generous approach to jurisprudence, with an openness to diverse theoretical perspectives demonstrated excellence in university teaching, curriculum development and higher degree research supervision outstanding communication, engagement and relationship-building skills, with the ability to cultivate influential scholarly partnerships nationally and internationally the capacity to contribute to public discourse and strengthen connections between jurisprudence, legal education and the legal profession. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa Sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration This is a full-time continuing opportunity. An attractive remuneration package will be offered. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sajad Bahram (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email at sajad.bahram@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Sunday 04 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Interaction-Design-Practice_0156887-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Interaction Design Practice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.2 FTE), 2 years fixed term position, located on the Camperdown Campus at The Sydney School of Architecture, Design and Planning Exciting opportunity to use your practice-based experience to contribute to teaching in the Discipline of Architecture Base Salary, level C $ 162,347 p.a. - $ 187,198 p.a., pro-rata + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Sydney School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) is an internationally acknowledged centre for intellectual and creative research, attracting the highest quality scholars in Australia and is committed to a sustainable future for Sydney and the world. Creatively combining the disciplines of Architecture, Urbanism and Design, we explore how cities, spaces, buildings, technologies, and services can better meet the needs of people and society. Our educators and researchers are experimental, imaginative, and critical, innovating at the boundaries of their disciplines and creating exciting and engaging learning experiences for our students – the global citizens of tomorrow. Design is a rapidly growing and highly successful discipline within the school. Our design research explores the role of design in speculating on the future, and to envision, define and inform the interactions and experiences between people and digital and emerging technologies. Pushing the boundaries of pedagogical practice in interaction and innovation, we encourage our students to critically rethink and prototype new objects, systems, experiences, and materials that shape human experience and life on the planet through the lens of design. Recognising that design cannot happen in isolation, we actively collaborate across disciplines and with external partners, including industry, not-for-profit, government and community groups and organisations. In particular, we value the importance of retaining strong ties to professional practice to make sure that the educational offering is up-to-date in a field that is rapidly changing. Reporting to Associate Professor Luke Hespanhol , your key responsibilities will be to: make independent, original and innovative contributions to research and education that expand knowledge or practice in the discipline teach across our growing design programs, including Bachelor of Design (Interaction Design), Master of Design, and Master of Interaction Design and Electronic Arts develop curriculum and delivery in whole or part of selected core and elective units of study, including design studios where appropriate. About you We are seeking an exceptional Senior Lecturer in Interaction Design Practice who has: a proven track record of original, high-quality and independent practice within the field of Interaction Design or UX/UI broad experience in teaching and learning at the university level, especially in Design studios demonstrated success in designing, implementing, and evaluating cutting-edge teaching and learning initiatives that inform curriculum design and development a collegial approach and willingness to contribute to mentoring others, academic governance, engagement, and administration a developing network of relationships with key academic, industry, and professional institutions, partners, and stakeholders. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Sydney/Venue-and-Programming-Manager_0155809-2": {
      "title": "Venue and Programming Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time Continuing position, located at the Sydney Conservatorium of Music, University of Sydney Contribute and manage overall operation of the Conservatorium rooms and performance venues, across the prestigious Sydney Conservatorium of Music Base Salary HEO 7, $117,133 p.a. + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Sydney Conservatorium of Music (SCM) is one of the oldest and most prestigious music schools in Australia. It is a distinctive part of Sydney’s arts and educational nucleus. The SCM is renowned for its exceptional commitment to music, innovative programs and partnership programs with Australia’s major music organisations. Since its inception in 1915, the Conservatorium has played an important role in shaping Sydney’s cultural identity. The Venue and Programming Manager has responsibility for the development, preparation and implementation of events staged at the SCM Parramatta location . They play a key role in the facilitation of public facing events and engagement with the broader Western Sydney community. The position requires flexible working hours with regular evening and weekend work and is responsible for providing a range of logistical and professional support to ensure the smooth and safe running of rehearsals, productions and special events. Your key responsibilities will be to: manage the delivery of daily administration activities and services, providing guidance to colleagues on the ongoing activities of the work area, ensuring high quality and efficient administrative support is provided in a pro-active manner to staff, students, clients and visitors develop opportunities with key stakeholders, arts organisations, local community groups, performers and musicians to program activations and performances in the venue an efficient high-quality communication/coordination and liaison point with staff in the University and key external stakeholders such as performers, clients and guests work closely with the Conservatoriums staff to program masterclasses, VIP events, special events, recitals, schools and community engagement and in the space provide sound policy and procedural advice to a range of stakeholders exercise and build collaborations, initiatives and judgement in resolving sometimes complex problems associated with key accountabilities and related to the work area work closely with SCM Parramatta technical team, develops strong competence with venue systems, maintaining staff user guides for a range of event scenarios identify opportunities and the development of event marketing materials including branded event invitations, post event surveys and digital advertising for SCM display screens manage payment and invoice management for contractors in liaison with the School’s Finance Officers, including budgeting, invoicing and debtor control. act as an additional specialist venue and event delivery resource for activities and events held at the Macquarie Street campus, Footbridge Theatre and other USYD venues when required. About you We are seeking an exceptional Venue and Programming Manager , who possesses: experience in venue management and event delivery with demonstratable skills in any number of disciplines including stage management, concert production, technical theatre operations, WHS delivery or FOH management.    tertiary qualifications in business administration or Arts, and/or equivalent relevant experience in arts administration previous experience in designing and delivering unique, and/or recurring artistic programs in a theatrical or concert environment the ability to identify and develop new relationships with potential users and hirers of the venue an understanding of audiences and how to build rapport with audiences to keep repeat visitation and high profile community presence experience in developing and implementing event schedules, coordinating performers and managing technical aspects of events in a musical or arts organisation preferred excellent interpersonal and communication skills, and providing excellent customer service ability to prioritise and manage multiple tasks to meet tight deadlines experience in financial management including managing budgets and controlling income and expenditure understanding of sound systems, IT infrastructure and event technology a driver’s licence would be preferrable. Work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Strategic-Design-Practice_0156890-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Strategic Design Practice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.2FTE), 2 years fixed term position, located on the Camperdown Campus at The Sydney School of Architecture, Design and Planning Exciting opportunity to use your practice-based experience to contribute to teaching in the Discipline of Design Base Salary, level C $ 162,347 p.a. - $ 187,198 p.a., pro-rata + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Sydney School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) is an internationally acknowledged centre for intellectual and creative research, attracting the highest quality scholars in Australia and is committed to a sustainable future for Sydney and the world. Creatively combining the disciplines of Architecture, Urbanism and Design, we explore how cities, spaces, buildings, technologies, and services can better meet the needs of people and society. Our educators and researchers are experimental, imaginative, and critical, innovating at the boundaries of their disciplines and creating exciting and engaging learning experiences for our students – the global citizens of tomorrow. Design is a rapidly growing and highly successful discipline within the school. Our design research explores the role of design in speculating on the future, and to envision, define and inform the interactions and experiences between people and digital and emerging technologies. Pushing the boundaries of pedagogical practice in interaction and innovation, we encourage our students to critically rethink and prototype new objects, systems, experiences, and materials that shape human experience and life on the planet through the lens of design. Recognising that design cannot happen in isolation, we actively collaborate across disciplines and with external partners, including industry, not-for-profit, government and community groups and organisations. In particular, we value the importance of retaining strong ties to professional practice to make sure that the educational offering is up-to-date in a field that is rapidly changing. Reporting to Associate Professor Luke Hespanhol , your key responsibilities will be to: make independent, original and innovative contributions to research and education that expand knowledge or practice in the discipline teach across our growing design programs, including Bachelor of Design (Interaction Design), Master of Design, and Master of Interaction Design and Electronic Arts develop curriculum and delivery in whole or part of selected core and elective units of study, including design studios where appropriate. About you We are seeking an exceptional Senior Lecturer in Strategic Design Practice who has: a proven track record of original, high-quality and independent practice within the field of Strategic Design broad experience in teaching and learning at the university level, especially in Design studios demonstrated success in designing, implementing, and evaluating cutting-edge teaching and learning initiatives that inform curriculum design and development a collegial approach and willingness to contribute to mentoring others, academic governance, engagement, and administration a developing network of relationships with key academic, industry, and professional institutions, partners, and stakeholders. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 17 September 2026 11:59 PM"
