    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow-in-Sedimentology-and-Biogeochemistry_0156315-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Sedimentology and Biogeochemistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 3-year fixed term opportunity, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Work on one of the world's most ambitious Earth system modelling initiatives Base Salary, Academic level A/B $117,936 - $157,381 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity Join the Quantitative Earth Systems (QuESt) Lab within the School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney and contribute to EarthLab, an innovative research initiative delivered in partnership with BHP. EarthLab is developing the world's most comprehensive whole-Earth modelling framework, integrating plate tectonics, mantle convection, landscape evolution, ocean-atmosphere-cryosphere interactions and biosphere processes to better understand the formation and preservation of critical mineral deposits. As Postdoctoral Research Associate / Research Fellow in Sedimentology and Biogeochemistry, you will play a key role in advancing our understanding of sedimentary mineral systems, particularly sediment-hosted copper deposits. Working alongside leading researchers and industry partners, you will contribute to cutting-edge research with direct relevance to global mineral exploration while helping to develop next-generation community research tools. Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with QuESt Lab researchers and BHP Exploration teams to advance mineral systems research develop and apply innovative approaches to sedimentological and biogeochemical investigations contribute to modelling sedimentary copper systems and Earth surface processes analyse and interpret large-scale geospatial and geochemical datasets publish high-quality research outcomes in leading international journals supervise honours and postgraduate student research projects where appropriate. This is an exceptional opportunity to be part of a globally significant research program tackling fundamental questions about Earth's evolution and mineral systems. You will: contribute to world-leading Earth systems science collaborate with internationally recognised researchers and industry partners publish impactful research with real-world applications access a vibrant, multidisciplinary geoscience research environment help build transformative scientific tools for the global research community. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate / Research Fellow who has: a PhD (or thesis submitted and close to completion) in sedimentology, sedimentary geochemistry, biogeochemistry, sedimentary-hosted mineral deposits or a closely related discipline demonstrated expertise in at least two of the following areas: sedimentology, sedimentary geochemistry, biogeochemistry, sedimentary-hosted mineral deposits or geospatial data analysis experience applying geological and geochemical datasets to understand Earth surface or mineral-system processes proficiency in scientific programming using Python or similar languages, or the demonstrated ability to rapidly develop coding capability a strong publication record relative to career stage in relevant research fields expertise in landscape evolution modelling and/or biogeochemical modelling using open-source tools will be highly regarded. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 01 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Geoscience-Modelling-Engineer_0156617": {
      "title": "Geoscience Modelling Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart-time (0.5 FTE), 3 years fixed term, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Work on one of the world's most ambitious Earth system modelling initiatives Base Salary, HEO10 $162,528 (pro rata) p.a + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for a Geoscience Modelling Engineer to join the QuESt (Quantitative Earth Systems) Lab within the School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney as a Geoscience Modelling Engineer. Working within the groundbreaking EarthLab program, delivered in partnership with BHP, you will contribute to the development of the world's most comprehensive whole-Earth modelling framework designed to improve understanding of how Earth systems control the formation and preservation of copper deposits. This role offers a unique opportunity to work at the forefront of computational geoscience, collaborating with leading researchers and industry partners to develop advanced modelling tools that integrate plate tectonics, mantle convection, landscape evolution, climate systems and biosphere processes into a unified computational framework. Your key responsibilities will be to: develop and maintain advanced geoscience modelling software and code frameworks contribute to the delivery of the EarthLab whole-Earth system modelling environment collaborate with EarthLab researchers and BHP Exploration teams on modelling initiatives support the integration of geodynamic, climate and landscape evolution models enhance and maintain community research tools used by the geoscience community contribute to research outputs and innovation in quantitative Earth systems science assist HDR students, researchers and postdoctoral staff with modelling workflows and code development. This position provides a rare opportunity to: work on a globally significant research initiative with direct application to mineral exploration partner with leading academic researchers and industry experts contribute to high-impact scientific discoveries and publications develop cutting-edge computational tools that will be used by the international geoscience community be part of a vibrant and collaborative research environment at Australia's leading university. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Geoscience Modelling Engineer who has: a PhD (or near completion) in computational geodynamics, tectonics, landscape evolution, palaeoclimate science or a closely related discipline, or equivalent industry experience demonstrated experience developing and maintaining geoscience modelling software expertise in one or more numerical geoscience areas including plate tectonic reconstructions, thermomechanical modelling, palaeoclimate modelling and/or landscape evolution modelling strong scientific programming capability in Python, C/C++ or similar programming languages a proven record of publishing and developing geoscience modelling codes experience working with complex computational modelling frameworks and large geoscience datasets a demonstrated ability to support collaborative research programs and multidisciplinary teams. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Research-Fellow-Daffodil-Centre_0156995-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow-Daffodil Centre",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.60 or 0.80 FTE) fixed term for six months (commencing 01/01/2027 ending 30/06/2027)- possibility of extension subject to funding Research Fellow role supporting Melanoma Research Base Salary $ 132,533 (pro rata 0.60 FTE) + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Daffodil Centre is a world-leading cancer research centre, directly influencing policy decisions and clinical practice across the cancer control continuum. The Daffodil Centre combines the complementary strengths of Cancer Council NSW and the University of Sydney to accelerate progress in cancer care and outcomes across the population. The partnership has created an unparalleled centre of national and international research excellence where research leads to real-world improvements to reduce the burden of cancer in the community. We are seeking a Research Fellow to join the Melanoma and Skin Cancer research team at the Daffodil Centre, on 6-month fixed-term research-focused position. The Melanoma and Skin Cancer team , led by Professor Anne Cust , conducts collaborative, multidisciplinary research with a public health focus. The team has strong links with Melanoma Institute Australia. This role will play a key part in supporting a melanoma clinical guidelines project, undertaking evidence synthesis activities that contribute to the compilation and development of clinical practice guidelines for melanoma management. The successful candidate will be an expert in systematic reviews and evidence synthesis, with significant experience producing high-quality evidence summaries and translating research evidence into clinical and policy-relevant outputs. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead and conduct evidence syntheses, systematic reviews and meta-analyses to support melanoma and skin cancer research projects undertake evidence review activities that contribute to the development and compilation of melanoma clinical management guidelines critically appraise scientific literature and translate findings into high-quality evidence summaries and guideline recommendations conduct original research independently and collaboratively, developing publications and conference presentations contribute to research funding applications, grant proposals and impact activities build and maintain collaborative relationships with researchers, clinicians and stakeholder groups support the translation of research findings into policy and clinical practice improvements assist with supervision, mentoring and training of postgraduate students, research assistants and early-career researchers actively contribute to the strategic objectives and collaborative culture of the Daffodil Centre About you completed a PhD in epidemiology or a related discipline extensive experience in evidence synthesis, systematic reviews and meta-analyses supporting melanoma research and skin cancer research recognised expertise in systematic review methodology and evidence synthesis approaches demonstrated track record of peer-reviewed publications arising from evidence synthesis and research activities experience supporting the development of clinical guidelines, policy recommendations or evidence-informed decision-making processes will be highly regarded strong understanding of epidemiological methods, research design and critical appraisal techniques proven ability to collaborate effectively with multidisciplinary teams, clinicians and external stakeholders experience supervising, mentoring and supporting the development of students, research assistants or early-career researchers excellent written and verbal communication skills, including scientific writing and presenting research findings to diverse audiences advanced organisational, analytical and project management skills with a high level of attention to detail Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 29 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer---Neuroscience-Physiology_0156620-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer – Neuroscience/Physiology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until October 2027  Exciting opportunity to be a Lecturer (Level B) within the School of Medical Sciences Base Salary starting from $132,533 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for Neuroscience and Physiology education. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of neuroscientists and medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas. The School of Medical Sciences is seeking a Lecturer (Level B) – Neuroscience/Physiology to make significant contributions to teaching and learning while engaging in educational leadership. The role will contribute to the design, delivery, evaluation and continuous improvement of high-quality educational experiences that support student learning outcomes and align with University and Faculty expectations. Working collaboratively with academic colleagues, the Lecturer will contribute to curriculum development, innovative teaching practices and educational initiatives across Neuroscience and Physiology. The role will also undertake scholarly activity, develop research skills and contribute to the advancement of discipline knowledge, particularly as it relates to teaching and learning practices. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake independent teaching in Neuroscience and Physiology, contributing to outstanding educational design, delivery, evaluation and outcomes engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences that meet University expectations and standards for education deliver quality units of study, learning activities and curriculum content that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements contribute research or scholarly activity as part of a team, contributing to discipline knowledge, particularly in relation to teaching and learning within the discipline engage in co-supervision of research students in accordance with School, Faculty and University expectations apply scholarship and knowledge of contemporary pedagogical practice to innovate and enhance education practices undertake academic administration activities that support the delivery of high-quality teaching and learning coordinate activities of academic staff and provide training, guidance and mentorship to less experienced academic staff contribute to a positive workplace culture that values diversity of thought, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes contribute to the strategic priorities of the University, Faculty and School engage beyond the University through conferences, seminars, outreach activities and professional networks undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required. About you PhD in a relevant field demonstrated understanding of the subject matter of Neuroscience and Physiology experience contributing to original research and engaging in scholarly activity experience in teaching and learning, including participation in team teaching and delivery across a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives developing networks with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience supervising or mentoring students, and colleagues strong planning and organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities excellent communication skills with the ability to engage effectively with students, academic colleagues and stakeholders commitment to innovative teaching practices, educational excellence and continuous improvement. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 28 September 2026 11:59 PM"
