    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer---Neuroscience-Medical-Sciences_0156619-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer – Neuroscience/Medical Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart time (0.5FTE), fixed term until October 2027 Exciting opportunity to be a Lecturer within the School of Medical Sciences Base Salary starting from $132,533 pro rata + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for Neuroscience and Medical Sciences education. With over 100 years of excellence in education, we are proud to be training the next generation of neuroscientists and medical scientists who will be leading members of the medical research community with an innovative integrated curriculum. Our team of researchers lead key internationally competitive research from molecules to medicines, from patient to policy across a range of therapeutic areas. The School of Medical Sciences is seeking a Lecturer (Level B) – Neuroscience/Medical Sciences to make significant contributions to teaching and learning while engaging in educational leadership. The role will contribute to the design, delivery, evaluation and continuous improvement of high-quality educational experiences that support student learning outcomes and align with University and Faculty expectations. Working collaboratively with academic colleagues, the Lecturer will contribute to curriculum development, innovative teaching practices and educational initiatives across Neuroscience and Medical Sciences. The role will also undertake scholarly activity, develop research skills and contribute to the advancement of discipline knowledge, particularly as it relates to teaching and learning practices. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake independent teaching in Neuroscience and Medical Sciences, contributing to outstanding educational design, delivery, evaluation and outcomes engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences that meet University expectations and standards for education deliver quality units of study, learning activities and curriculum content that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements contribute research or scholarly activity as part of a team, contributing to discipline knowledge, particularly in relation to teaching and learning within the discipline engage in co-supervision of research students in accordance with School, Faculty and University expectations apply scholarship and knowledge of contemporary pedagogical practice to innovate and enhance education practices undertake academic administration activities that support the delivery of high-quality teaching and learning coordinate activities of academic staff and provide training, guidance and mentorship to less experienced academic staff contribute to a positive workplace culture that values diversity of thought, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes contribute to the strategic priorities of the University, Faculty and School engage beyond the University through conferences, seminars, outreach activities and professional networks undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required. About you PhD in a relevant field demonstrated understanding of the subject matter of Neuroscience and Medical Sciences experience contributing to original research and engaging in scholarly activity experience in teaching and learning, including participation in team teaching and delivery across a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives developing networks with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience supervising or mentoring students, and colleagues strong planning and organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities excellent communication skills with the ability to engage effectively with students, academic colleagues and stakeholders commitment to innovative teaching practices, educational excellence and continuous improvement. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact, Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 28 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Associate-Vice-President--Sydney-Future-Students_0157138-1": {
      "title": "Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUniversity of Sydney The University of Sydney is seeking an exceptional leader to join its External Engagement portfolio as Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students. Reporting to the Vice-President, External Engagement, this influential executive role provides strategic leadership of the University's student recruitment and admissions functions and plays a critical role in delivering the University's growth ambitions, student experience objectives and revenue goals.  The Opportunity Higher education is undergoing significant transformation. Changing student expectations, increasing domestic and international competition, advances in artificial intelligence and digital engagement, evolving government policy settings and demographic shifts are reshaping the future student landscape. As Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students, you will lead the University's student recruitment and admissions strategy, ensuring the University continues to attract and enrol outstanding students from Australia and around the world. Working closely with senior leaders across the institution, you will provide strategic leadership and market insights to support decision-making, strengthen competitive positioning and drive sustainable growth. This is a rare opportunity to lead one of the most strategically important functions within Australia's leading university, with responsibility for shaping the future student pipeline and contributing directly to the University's long-term success. Key Responsibilities Working closely with the Vice-President, External Engagement, Deans, Deputy Vice-Chancellors and senior leaders across the University, you will: Lead the development and execution of the University's student recruitment and admissions strategy. Drive domestic and international recruitment performance, with significant responsibility for University revenue, while balancing sustainable growth with the University's broader strategic priorities. Provide strategic advice and market intelligence to inform institutional planning and decision-making. Lead the delivery of a high-performing, service-oriented student recruitment and admissions function. Utilise market insights, competitive intelligence and emerging trends to identify growth opportunities and strengthen market position. Develop and maintain strong partnerships across faculties, portfolios and external stakeholders. Lead organisational performance, financial planning, governance, risk management and compliance across the portfolio. Influence policy development and strategic responses to changes in the higher education and regulatory environment. Champion continuous improvement and innovation to enhance the prospective student experience. Leadership Impact This role sits at the centre of the University's growth strategy and student experience agenda. The successful candidate will lead a significant and highly visible function responsible for recruitment, admissions and market intelligence, while building strong relationships across a complex stakeholder environment that includes Faculties, professional services leaders, external partners and government stakeholders. You will bring the credibility, influence and leadership capability required to align diverse stakeholders around shared objectives, drive organisational performance and deliver meaningful outcomes for students and the University. The portfolio also encompasses the University's global mobility and exchange function, providing an opportunity to shape and strengthen the University's international student experience and global engagement agenda. About You (skills, capabilities and experience) You are an accomplished executive leader with a track record of delivering strategic growth outcomes in a complex organisation. You will bring: Significant leadership experience in student recruitment, admissions, marketing, customer acquisition, commercial growth, student experience or a related field. A demonstrated ability to develop and execute large-scale growth and engagement strategies. Strong commercial and financial acumen, with experience managing budgets, performance targets and business planning. Exceptional stakeholder engagement and influencing skills. Experience leading large, complex teams through change and transformation. A strong understanding of data, analytics and market intelligence to support strategic decision-making. The ability to operate effectively in a highly complex and matrixed environment. Outstanding communication, leadership and organisational skills. While experience in higher education is highly regarded, applications are encouraged from candidates in adjacent sectors who have led large-scale customer acquisition, admissions, student engagement, commercial growth, marketing or service operations functions. Why Join the University of Sydney? As Australia's first university and one of the world's leading research-intensive institutions, the University of Sydney is committed to excellence in education, research and community impact. The Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students will play a pivotal role in shaping the University's future growth and student community. This position offers the opportunity to influence institutional strategy, lead a critical enterprise-wide function and contribute to the future of higher education at both a national and global level. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a continuing position on a full-time basis. The appointment is subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Dr Courtenay Atwell Korn Ferry Email: Courtenay.atwell@kornferry.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 13 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Technician-Commitment-Program-Officer_0156631-2": {
      "title": "Technician Commitment Program Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 2 years Unique opportunity to deliver the newly-established Technician Commitment in Australia HEO 6 Base Salary $107,312 - $115,686 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity Microscopy Australia is the national collaborative research infrastructure providing researchers with access to world class microscopy, imaging and characterisation capabilities. Through a network of leading facilities hosted by the University of Sydney and partner universities and research organisations across Australia, Microscopy Australia supports cutting edge research, innovation and industry engagement across fields ranging from health and medicine to materials science and environmental research. The Technician Commitment is an internationally recognised initiative driving systemic and cultural change for technical and scientific professionals across higher education, research and innovation. Supported by a major Australian Government investment, this team will support the establishment and delivery of the Technician Commitment in Australia in partnership with Microscopy Australia, the Academy for Collaborative Research Infrastructure (ACRI), and the UK Technician Commitment team. As the Technical Commitment Program Officer , you will play a critical role in ensuring the successful operational delivery of the program, supporting stakeholder engagement, governance, reporting, financial administration, events, and continuous improvement activities. This role sits within a newly established team that is building dedicated capability to support the technical workforce underpinning Australia's research excellence. The team will develop and deliver initiatives that enhance professional development opportunities, strengthen career pathways, and elevate the contribution of technical staff to research and innovation. This is a unique opportunity to help shape the future of the technical workforce across the Australian research sector. Your key responsibilities will be to: coordinate the operational delivery of the Technician Commitment in Australia, including program plans, milestones, reporting, records and continuous improvement activities support stakeholder engagement by managing enquiries, onboarding signatories and supporters, and maintaining effective relationships with delivery partners and sector stakeholders lead or coordinate projects, workstreams and national program activities, including events, workshops, meetings and engagement initiatives provide governance and administrative support, including secretariat services, self-assessment processes, action tracking and preparation of reports, briefings and presentations monitor program budgets, expenditure and financial reporting requirements, supporting forecasting and funding administration activities maintain accurate data, risk, issue and decision registers, reporting progress, achievements and emerging risks to the Program Lead About you demonstrated experience in program coordination, project administration or operational delivery, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and stakeholders experience developing and maintaining effective operational systems, reporting processes, governance documentation and records strong financial and administrative capability, including budget monitoring, reporting and data management excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with experience building productive relationships across diverse organisations and sectors experience coordinating meetings, committees, events, workshops or other stakeholder engagement activities, supported by strong attention to detail and organisational skills knowledge of the Australian higher education, research or research infrastructure sector, with a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion; understanding of technical professionals would be advantageous, alongside a relevant degree or equivalent professional experience. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Lena Jiang, Recruitment Operations by email to lena.jiang@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Executive-Director--Digital-Engagement_0157438-1": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Digital Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, Fixed term opportunity for 5 years Shape strategy and deliver initiatives that make a meaningful difference across the University. Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% super About the opportunity The Executive Director, Digital Engagement is part of the ICT Leadership Team which is responsible for the development of the University’s ICT strategy, plans and roadmaps. The Executive Director, Digital Engagement is responsible for overseeing ICT partnerships with the broader University and driving a focus on client-centricity. As a member of the ICT Leadership Team, this role will provide apply extensive management and professional expertise, skills and knowledge to contribute effectively to the development of the ICT Strategy, ensuring alignment with the University’s objectives and needs. As an Executive Director, this role is required make high-level strategic and operational decisions, understanding related implications, risks and opportunities. The role will provide strategic leadership, direction and oversight to the Digital Engagement function and collaborate closely and frequently with senior leadership across the ICT department as well as key University stakeholders Your key responsibilities will be to: Lead the ICT partnering function to engage, advise and support senior stakeholders, the Portfolio Service Units (PSUs) and Faculties across the University in the development of their digital strategies. Ensure the client technology strategy aligns with current and future digital technologies that underpin the ICT digital roadmap. Lead the development and implementation of the Digital Engagement strategy and plan(s), ensuring alignment with the broader ICT strategy / plan(s), including budget and resource allocation strategies and plans. Develop, submit and implement annual operational and capital budgets, manage expenditure effectively within budget Ensure the development and delivery of reports on progress and any challenges encountered. Lead and mentor a team of Engagement staff, fostering a collaborative and innovative work environment. Lead initiatives that promote a culture of continuous improvement across ICT. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) Minimum of 10 years of ICT and/or business leadership experience and 5+ years of management experience within a Client Engagement-related team / function Extensive management experience in an ICT function within the University or Education sector or a complex ICT environment. Significant experience in managing large and complex budgets and resource pools. Knowledge of tertiary sector issues, challenges and dynamics and their application to the ICT function Extensive experience with program and portfolio management Ability to effectively prioritise and execute tasks in high-pressure situations. Extensive experience leading strategic planning and technology roadmap development in alignment with broader organisational strategy. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Term and remuneration This is a full-time, fixed term opportunity for 5 years. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the position description for further details. Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Mushtaq Sobhan, Recruitment Operations by email to mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Wednesday 30 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Veterinary-Nurse---Emergency-Critical-Care_0153189-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Nurse - Emergency Critical Care",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing position, contingent on the continuation of external funding Located on the Camperdown Campus at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital, Sydney (UVTHS) Exciting opportunity to provide best practice veterinary care and student teaching as part of the emergency and critical care team, within a multi-disciplinary specialty hospital Base Salary HEO3, Step 1 (38 hpw), $82,388 p.a. + 17% superannuation + appropriate shift loadings About the opportunity The University of Sydney is welcoming applications for a qualified Veterinary Nurse in Emergency Critical Care to provide nursing and technical assistance with clinical and emergency cases presented to the small animal hospital. Your key responsibilities will be to:  provide excellent care to critical patients within the Critical Care Unit, including monitoring/nursing care of oxygen dependant and ventilated patients assist clinicians and veterinary student interns with treatments and emergency medical procedures, focusing on student learning and engagement participate in RECOVER-based CPR triage of incoming emergency patients, assist with stabilisation and managing workflow of vets/students provide support for clinical teaching and professional nursing assistance with clinical cases handle telephone and face-to-face enquiries, as well as after-hours reception duties maintain stock, including ordering of consumables, procurement of supplies, and control of stock quantities clean and maintain clinical spaces care for and maintain instruments and equipment. The small animal service at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney (UVTHS) offers: a dedicated 24-hour emergency and critical care service a small animal specialist veterinary practice for the management of a wide range of medical, surgical and emergent problems a first opinion general practice second opinion services access to advanced equipment and imaging. In addition to providing excellent patient care, UVTHS provides an invaluable teaching experience for our final-year veterinary students. You will work closely with Specialists, residents, and interns in a range of disciplines. To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science, click here About you The University values courage and creativity, openness and engagement, inclusion and diversity, and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned with these values and are looking for an ECC Veterinary Nurse who has: a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing (or equivalent) and/or advanced veterinary nursing experience in a small animal veterinary hospital emergency environment proficiency in general nursing skills, including placement of IV catheters, phlebotomy, performing radiographs of diagnostic quality, and anaesthesia for emergency patients (e.g. blocked cats/stitch-ups) knowledge of a large range of veterinary emergency and critical care cases demonstrated ability to cope with emergencies and stressful situations demonstrated ability to work independently and as a team the ability to communicate with veterinary professionals and academic staff, as well as confidence in a teaching role experience working in a customer service environment with demonstrated high levels of care for patients and clients a willingness and ability to work a rotating roster across weekdays, evenings, overnights, alternating weekends and public holidays demonstrated ability to lift animals up to 16kg and heavier animals with assistance (mechanical and/or team lifting), adhering to the University’s Heavy Lifting Policy vaccination for Q Fever or willingness to be vaccinated experience with advanced anaesthesia will be highly regarded. The key benefits: opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund yearly personal and professional development planning, including support for continuing education access to the employee assistance program access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres industry-leading leave provisions, including generous paid parental leave entitlements (pending eligibility up to 36 weeks) located on the Camperdown campus, 4 km from the city centre, in a convenient central location with ample public transport options. Discounted Staff Parking is also available on site. Base Salary Yearly salary reviews held up to HEO3 Step 5 Work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary steps, including the termination of your employment. EEO Statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence , and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page or click here For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 04 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Veterinary-Registrar-in-Equine-Sports-Medicine_0157001": {
      "title": "Veterinary Registrar in Equine Sports Medicine",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term Parental Leave Cover until 6th March 2028 Opportunity to work as part of a dynamic and growing equine team   Base Salary HEO8 (38 hrs p/w) $142,344 - $160,534 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity Reporting directly to the Head Equine Unit, the Veterinary Registrar in Equine Sports Medicine and Rehabilitation position will form part of the Camden Equine Centre  (CEC) within the UVTHC in providing equine sports medicine and rehabilitation services for the hospital. This position is designed to assist in developing and growing the equine unit’s referral practice, particularly performance horses, and participate in the clinical teaching of DVM students. This position is full time and covers some weekend and after-hours duty, following a rota with all clinicians within the equine unit for ambulatory work and inpatients. Your key responsibilities will be to:  provide a specialist level sports medicine service for primary accession, secondary and tertiary referral cases grow the client base by excellent client and referring practice communications and service oversee the supervision or postgraduate /clinical master students mentoring of junior clinical staff provide clinical instruction to DVM students in the areas of general and specialist practice co-ordinate and provide professional guidance to non-specialist equine clinicians in relation to cases. CEC is recognised as a centre of clinical excellence for equine sports medicine in Australasia and a leader in the diagnosis, treatment and rehabilitation of horses with sports related injuries and illnesses through innovative research in the field whilst training the veterinarians of the future.  To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here About you a Veterinary Degree registrable in NSW postgraduate qualifications in sports medicine and rehabilitation more than 12 months experience following a residency program but have not yet achieved specialist status OR may have membership of the ANZCVS or similar post graduate level qualifications as well as additional advanced training excellent equine clinical skills an ability to develop referral and primary accession practice an ability to provide clinical instruction to postgraduate and DVM students a willingness to mentor junior clinical staff and provide clinical instruction to postgraduate and undergraduate students. The key benefits be stimulated by an academic environment focused on teaching and research opportunity for you to learn and enhance your skills and experience in a unique teaching and research environment of a veterinary teaching hospital salary packaging available access to employee assistance programs (EAP) at no cost access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 04 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Academic-Development-and-Leadership--Education-Focused-_0156446": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Academic Development and Leadership (Education Focused)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n2x Full time, fixed term for 12 months, with potential for extension Unique opportunity for a talented educator to contribute to the university’s Teaching and Learning strategic plan Academic Level B, Base Salary $132,533 – $147,445 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The University of Sydney seeks to deliver a transformational learning experience for its students and a culture of teaching for academics. To support these strategic aims, we are recruiting a fantastic opportunity for a Lecturer in Academic Development and Leadership (Education Focused) within our Division of Teaching and Learning team in the DVC Education & Students Portfolio. Education-focused roles are a specialised category of academic engagement reserved for talented educators with a passion for, and demonstrated excellence in, pedagogical practice and design. In this role you will be expected to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation within the program and will be on a trajectory towards leadership in educational design and delivery. The majority of your contribution will be within education (70%), with a proportion of workload (30%) available for research and service. Your key responsibilities can include: work within the Academic Development team to contribute to the delivery of educational programs and experiences that support staff and student needs, particularly around generative AI contribute to assurance of learning initiatives contribute to professional development courses for educators, with a particular focus on providing high-quality feedback and assessment of participant work apply scholarship and knowledge of contemporary pedagogical practice to educational innovation develop and suggest innovative practices in teaching and learning with a view to improving educational outcomes demonstrate impact through improving student outcomes, experiences and learning. We will be hiring two positions, one around AI professional learning and a second around assurance of learning, and so the above responsibilities will vary by role. About you experience with academic development and the support of other educators and their practice experience supporting the development of educators' teaching practice, including individual consultation, group facilitation, and the design of scalable professional learning initiatives proven ability to work effectively as a member of a team with colleagues from differing roles and disciplinary backgrounds on strategic projects at an institutional level high-level oral and written communication skills and the ability to lead and influence success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives, preferably in a tertiary academic development context passion and expertise for, and excellence in, university teaching and academic development expertise in the effective use of technology in teaching at the tertiary level strong attention to detail and deadlines, as well as the ability to accommodate changing work priorities awarded Fellowship of the Higher Education Academy UK or higher a PhD or other postgraduate qualifications in Higher Education or similar is desirable. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 11 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Westmead-Campus/Research-Fellow_0156812": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart-time fixed-term opportunity (0.4 FTE) for up to 2 years for a dedicated Research Fellow Be part of innovative neurological and translational research at the Children’s Hospital Academic Level B $132,533 (Pro rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking a Research Fellow to join a leading research program based at the Children's Hospital, working under the supervision of Professor Russell Dale. This role will focus on developing metabolomic assays for the discovery and translation of novel metabolites in cerebrospinal fluid and plasma, contributing to impactful research in neurological and biomedical sciences. Working as part of a collaborative research environment, you will apply and refine your expertise in research methodologies, design and delivery while contributing to the University's reputation for research excellence. Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct original research independently and collaboratively, with a focus on high-quality publications and research outcomes develop and optimise metabolomic assays for the analysis of cerebrospinal fluid and plasma samples contribute to competitive research grant applications and funding opportunities establish and maintain productive research collaborations and translational research partnerships disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, conferences, and seminars support research excellence through maintaining quality standards and contributing to a positive research culture provide mentoring and guidance to students and junior researchers where appropriate. About you PhD or equivalent doctoral qualification in a relevant scientific discipline demonstrated experience conducting original research and scholarly activities experience or strong interest in metabolomics, biomarker discovery, neuroscience, biomedical science or a related field track record of research outputs, including publications and conference presentations ability to build effective collaborations with academic, industry and professional stakeholders strong communication, planning, organisational and presentation skills demonstrated ability to work effectively both independently and within a multidisciplinary research team. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 01 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Westmead-Campus/Senior-Research-Fellow_0156814-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart-time fixed-term opportunity (0.4 FTE) for 12 months, with funding available for up to 2 years for a Senior Research Fellow Join a leading translational research program focused on drug discovery and therapeutic development Academic Level C $162,347 (Pro rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for a Senior Research Fellow to join a high-performing research team within the University of Sydney. Working at the intersection of chemistry and biomedical research, you will play a key role in advancing short-chain fatty acid chemistry, pro-drug development, and the discovery of novel therapeutic approaches for future animal and human applications. This position offers the opportunity to lead innovative research programs, build collaborations across academia and industry, and contribute to the translation of scientific discoveries into meaningful health outcomes. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead and conduct independent research in drug discovery, medicinal chemistry, and therapeutic development develop and advance research programs focused on short-chain fatty acid chemistry and pro-drug development publish high-quality research outcomes in leading academic journals and present findings at national and international conferences secure competitive research funding and contribute to the growth of externally funded research activities establish and maintain strategic collaborations with academic, industry and professional partners mentor and guide junior researchers and contribute to the development of a positive research culture contribute to research governance, strategic initiatives, and broader academic activities within the University support the translation and commercialisation of research outcomes where appropriate. About you PhD in Chemistry, Medicinal Chemistry, Pharmaceutical Sciences, or a closely related discipline significant experience conducting original research and developing a strong research profile demonstrated expertise in short-chain fatty acid chemistry, drug discovery, and/or pro-drug development track record of high-quality publications and scholarly achievement experience securing or contributing to competitive research funding applications established collaborative networks across academic, industry or professional sectors demonstrated experience mentoring and supervising research staff or students excellent communication skills with the ability to engage diverse stakeholders and research partners. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. As this position is located on an NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a university community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Danielle Selinger Recruitment Consultant by email to danielle.selinger@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 01 October 2026 11:59 PM"
